window.__hvBdRegister("proclus-timaeus", {"key":"proclus-timaeus","sections":[{"i":0,"j":64,"loc":"Introduction; Names of the Philosophers Quoted by Proclus","title":"Translator's introduction and list of philosophers","summary":"The translator presents Proclus as the greatest link in the golden chain of Platonic interpreters and regrets that the commentary survives only for about a third of the Timæus. He lists what the surviving part covers: the causes of the universe, the world's body and soul, partial souls, the elements and the heavens. He then defends the later Platonists against the charge of corrupting Plato, blaming critics who lacked mathematics, logic and knowledge of Plato's veiled manner of writing. He describes his own translation and his more than eleven hundred emendations, and a list follows of the philosophers Proclus cites, each with a short identification and date.","ideas":["Proclus as the chief interpreter of Plato among the later Platonists","The surviving commentary covers only part of the Timæus","Critics of the later Platonists lacked the training Plato's philosophy demands","The translator holds that Plato concealed his highest doctrines from the many"],"terms":[{"t":"latter Platonists","d":"Plotinus and his successors down to the closing of the schools under Justinian, whom the translator defends."},{"t":"Olympiad","d":"The four-year period by which the Greeks reckoned dates, used in the list of philosophers."}]},{"i":65,"j":73,"loc":"Book I (prologue) (1 of 2)","title":"The scope, causes and character of the Timæus","summary":"Proclus holds that the Timæus covers the whole of physiology and follows the Pythagorean treatise of Timæus the Locrian. Unlike earlier and later physicists, who dealt with matter or with matter and form, Plato alone treats these as concauses and investigates the true causes: the maker, the paradigm and the good. The dialogue opens with images of the universe (the summary of the polity and the Atlantic war), sets out the fabrication of the whole in the middle, and ends with partial natures and man, the microcosm; Aristotle is said to follow this plan but stop at matter. Its style mixes Pythagorean elevation with Socratic demonstration. Proclus closes by summarizing the setting and making Timæus the image of the Demiurgus and the three listeners the image of the demiurgic triad.","ideas":["Matter and form are concauses; the efficient, paradigmatic and final causes are causes proper","The polity and the Atlantic war present the cosmos through images","Man as microcosm is studied within the theory of the universe","The persons of the dialogue mirror the Demiurgus and a demiurgic triad"],"terms":[{"t":"concause","d":"A contributing condition such as matter or form that serves the true causes of generation."},{"t":"Demiurgus","d":"The divine maker or fabricating intellect of the universe."},{"t":"physiology","d":"The study of nature, which Proclus takes to be the whole scope of the dialogue."}]},{"i":74,"j":82,"loc":"Book I (prologue) (2 of 2)","title":"What nature is; Timæus and Parmenides","summary":"Proclus reviews earlier definitions of nature as matter, form, physical powers, the art of God or soul, and gives Plato's view: nature is an incorporeal essence between soul and corporeal powers, inseparable from bodies, holding their productive principles but unable to turn back on itself. He places it in a series running from the intelligible paradigm through the demiurgic intellect and soul down to nature and the sensible, and calls it the last cause that fabricates the sensible world, suspended from the vivific Goddess. He rejects Amelius's or Theodorus's three demiurgi beyond soul, and calls nature a proceeding art and organ of the Gods. With Iamblichus he holds that the Timæus, on mundane things, and the Parmenides, on intelligibles, together contain the whole of Plato's theory.","ideas":["Nature lies between soul and corporeal powers","Nature is the last of the causes fabricating the sensible world","Art is threefold: abiding in the artist, abiding and proceeding, proceeding only","The Timæus and Parmenides divide philosophy between them"],"terms":[{"t":"physis (nature)","d":"For Proclus, an incorporeal life inseparable from bodies that contains and governs their productive principles."},{"t":"reasons (logoi)","d":"The productive principles through which nature forms and governs bodies."}]},{"i":83,"j":89,"loc":"Book I, lemma: \"One, two, three, but where…the fourth\"","title":"The opening count of one, two, three","summary":"On Socrates' opening count, Proclus reports Praxiphanes' objections to the counting and to the shift to the word fourth, and Porphyry's replies that the count points out those present and marks the absent one. Proclus insists the dialogue is Pythagorean and reads the words at three levels. Ethically they show Pythagorean friendship, kept compacts and shared doctrine, and a feast of discourse rather than of the body. Physically the numbers are participated numbers in things; theologically the monad, duad and triad signify the final, paradigmatic and producing causes, and the banquet images the Gods filling one another.","ideas":["Praxiphanes' grammatical objections answered by Porphyry","Ethical, physical and theological readings of one passage","Monad, duad and triad stand for the good, the paradigm and the maker","The banquet of discourse images the Gods' mutual feasting"],"terms":[{"t":"monad, duad, triad","d":"The first numbers, which Proclus links to the final, paradigmatic and producing causes."}]},{"i":90,"j":110,"loc":"Book I, lemmas: \"a certain infirmity has befallen him\" to \"return the hospitality\"","title":"The absent fourth listener and the return feast","summary":"Proclus surveys explanations for the fourth person's absence: Porphyry sees an ethical lesson about illness and friends' apologies, and Iamblichus holds the absent one was too lofty for physics. Guesses that he was Theætetus, Clitophon or Plato are set aside. Syrianus' view, which Proclus adopts, is that hearers grow fewer as the discussion grows more mystical, so the absent man was inferior, not superior, to those present. Socrates asking the others to fill his place, and Timæus' promise to repay the feast, are read as images of the Demiurgus filling every deficiency and of the Gods' reciprocal giving. A note dates the Republic and Timæus to the Bendidian festival and the day after.","ideas":["Fewer hearers for more elevated discussions","The absent man's weakness was inferiority, not transcendence","Diminished number as a sign of greater power","Reciprocal feasting images the communion of divine powers"],"terms":[{"t":"Syrianus","d":"Proclus' teacher, whose interpretation he usually adopts as closest to Plato."}]},{"i":111,"j":124,"loc":"Book I, lemmas: \"Do you remember…\" to \"separate husbandmen…from the belligerent genus\"","title":"Recalling the polity and the division of classes","summary":"Timæus remembers some things and asks Socrates to recall the rest, which Proclus reads as a mean between irony and arrogance and as an image of productive principles that persist yet are renewed. He explains why the polity is narrated a third time and argues that the dispute over whether its topic is the polity or justice is empty, since justice in the soul and the polity in the city are the same thing. Against Longinus and Origen he holds that the summary covers the whole polity, not just the first or middle one. Socrates' separation of farmers and artisans from the warrior class is mapped onto the heavens and generation, onto the soul's three parts and onto the planetary Gods.","ideas":["Memory in the Demiurgus versus partial recollection","Justice in the soul and the polity in the city coincide","The polity is both below and above physiology","City classes correspond to Gods, dæmons and mortals and to the planets"],"terms":[{"t":"Mnemosyne","d":"Memory as it exists in the Demiurgus, the stable establishment of divine intelligence."}]},{"i":125,"j":147,"loc":"Book I, lemmas: \"one employment only\" to \"pay attention to virtue alone\"","title":"Guardians' tasks, education and common life","summary":"Proclus explains why each citizen follows one art suited to his nature and how this holds among the Gods, each of whom is all things after his own peculiarity. The guardians, mild to friends and fierce to enemies, image powers that deal gently with souls akin to the intelligible and severely with the disorder of matter; philosophic and irascible natures correspond to Gods and dæmons. Their education in music, gymnastic and the sciences corresponds to the harmony of celestial lives, the rhythm of their vehicles and their contemplation of intelligibles. Their lack of private gold and silver, and their wages from those they protect, are read as the self-sufficiency of the Gods, the metals as effluxions of the planets, and the exchange of effluxions and exhalations between heaven and earth.","ideas":["One work for each nature, mirrored in divine peculiarities","External and internal enemies as the disorder of matter and souls","Music, gymnastic and mathematics in the universe","The Gods need nothing yet receive our thanks and conversion"],"terms":[{"t":"auxiliaries and guardians","d":"The two warrior ranks of the city, fighting with hands and with counsel respectively."},{"t":"Adrastia","d":"The divine law whose sacred institutions dæmons are said to preserve."}]},{"i":148,"j":166,"loc":"Book I, lemmas: \"Of women too…\" to \"those…proved afterwards bad, should be…banished\"","title":"Women, common marriages and the transfer of children","summary":"Proclus endorses Plato's view that men and women share one human form and therefore the same virtues, and finds this mirrored in paired male and female deities, from Jupiter and Juno down to dæmons. The common marriages and children, and the sacred marriages arranged by secret lots so that like is joined with like, are read as the communion of all Gods and the unseen causes that pair natures of equal rank. Against Longinus' doubt whether souls come with the seed, Syrianus answers that nature prepares bodies fit for better or worse souls. The sending of bad children to the lower city and the recall of good ones are read as souls' descents into generation and their ascents.","ideas":["Men and women share one form and one virtue","Divine communion as the model for common marriages","Bodies are prepared as fitting receptacles for souls","Descent and return of partial souls imaged in the transfer of children"],"terms":[{"t":"sacred marriages","d":"Mystic unions of Gods which Plato's marriage arrangements are said to imitate."},{"t":"partial souls","d":"Individual souls like ours, which descend into generation and ascend again."}]},{"i":167,"j":177,"loc":"Book I, lemmas: \"Have we…sufficiently resumed the epitome\" to \"beautiful animals…in motion\"","title":"Socrates wishes to see his city in motion","summary":"Socrates asks whether his summary is complete, and Proclus treats the epitome as a picture of how the universe is filled with productive powers. He lists the five points that follow: what Socrates desires, his own inability, the inability of poets and of sophists, and the fitness of the present company. Porphyry explains the wish to see the city at war as energy perfecting habit; Proclus objects that this is Peripatetic and refers the wish to the Demiurgus, who delights in seeing the world move and governs the war of generation. The painted or living animals at rest are read as divine beings full of intellectual order, while Longinus' and Origen's remarks on the diction are noted and answered.","ideas":["The epitome images the ordering of the universe","Porphyry's energy-perfects-habit reading rejected as Peripatetic","The Demiurgus delights in seeing the world move","Animals at rest and in motion as divine natures and their processions"],"terms":[{"t":"junior or second fabrication","d":"The lower demiurgic order that governs motion, contrariety and the war of generation."}]},{"i":178,"j":192,"loc":"Book I, lemmas: \"contests of our city\" to \"The genus…of your habit remains\"","title":"Why Socrates, poets and sophists cannot praise","summary":"Socrates wants the city praised for deeds and words in war. Proclus rejects explanations of Socrates' inability based on style, inexperience of war or irony, and holds that Socrates avoids descending to a third remove from the truth, an inability that is really abundance of power. On the poets, Origen's three-day distress over whether Homer is included is answered by distinguishing inspired from artificial poetry; only imitative poetry nourished in bad manners is rejected. Imitating words is harder than imitating deeds because it demands a like disposition. Sophists, skilled but wandering, also fail, so the task falls to those who are both philosophers and politicians, images of total demiurgic causes.","ideas":["Socrates stays with the paradigm rather than descend to its third image","Inspired versus artificial and imitative poetry","Imitating words requires a life like the speaker's","Only philosopher-politicians can portray the best city in action"],"terms":[{"t":"mimēsis (imitation)","d":"Poetic representation, which succeeds only for what the imitator has been educated in."}]},{"i":193,"j":206,"loc":"Book I, lemmas: \"Timæus here of Locris\" to \"I assent to your compliance\"","title":"Praise of the speakers and their consent","summary":"Socrates praises Timæus as statesman and summit of philosophy, which Proclus reads as an image of the Demiurgus, royal and full of the first intelligibles. Critias, despite his later tyranny, is praised and assigned to the middle fabrication, and Hermocrates, the Syracusan general, to the third and last; an alternative scheme assigning the four persons to paradigmatic, efficient, formal and material causes is noted. Socrates declares himself ready for the promised feast, as Jupiter in Homer stays on Olympus and sends lower Gods to war. Hermocrates speaks so as not to be a mute figure and calls on Critias, who defers to Timæus; all looking to Timæus images all demiurgic causes depending on the one father.","ideas":["Timæus as the image of the Demiurgus","Critias and Hermocrates as middle and last fabrication","Socrates rests in the form of the polity while others narrate its motion","Secondary causes call forth and defer to primary ones"],"terms":[{"t":"Locri","d":"Timæus' Italian city, famed for the laws of Zaleucus."}]},{"i":207,"j":230,"loc":"Book I, lemmas: \"a narration surprising…yet…true\" to \"celebrating her divinity…with hymns\"","title":"How to read the Atlantic story","summary":"Proclus surveys readings of the Atlantic narrative: Crantor takes it as plain history, others as pure fable, and others as images of cosmic oppositions. Amelius maps the war onto fixed stars and planets, Origen onto dæmons, Numenius onto souls, and Porphyry onto souls and evil dæmons. With Iamblichus and Syrianus, Proclus accepts it as true history and extends its contrariety through the whole cosmos, citing the Gods' battles in Homer and noting that Plato puts the war among men out of piety. He then discusses Solon as wisest of the seven, the tripod story, and Plato's descent from Dropides, and reads the Minerva festival, the lesser Panathenæa, as an image of the Goddess ordering cosmic opposition.","ideas":["The story is history and also an image of cosmic contrariety","Rival allegories by Amelius, Origen, Numenius and Porphyry","Contrariety pervades Gods, dæmons, souls and bodies","The Panathenæa and Minerva's veil as images of ordered opposition"],"terms":[{"t":"two co-ordinations","d":"The paired opposite series of things running through the cosmos."},{"t":"Panathenæa","d":"The Athenian festival of Minerva; the Timæus is set at the lesser and the Parmenides at the greater."}]},{"i":231,"j":251,"loc":"Book I, lemmas: \"what is this ancient achievement\" to \"Relate this affair…from the beginning\"","title":"Critias' boyhood memory and Solon's poetry","summary":"Socrates asks about the deed, and Critias explains he heard it as a boy of ten from his ninety-year-old grandfather at the Apaturia. Proclus rejects Longinus' purely stylistic remarks in favour of Origen's and Iamblichus' readings and describes the festival's three days and the boys' recitation contests. He reads the chain of Solon, the elder Critias and the younger as causes of permanence, procession and conversion, and the festival and rhapsodies as images of souls woven into the life of the universe. Amynander's praise of Solon as a poet and the elder Critias' remark that sedition kept Solon from finishing his poem are read both as narrative safeguards for the story's reliability and as images of matter's disorder hindering higher causes.","ideas":["Longinus judged a philologist, not a philosopher","The Apaturia and its contests explained","Solon, the elder and the younger Critias as a triad of causes","Why the messengers of the story could neither deceive nor be deceived"],"terms":[{"t":"Apaturia","d":"Festival of Bacchus at which boys were enrolled in their tribes on the day called Cureotis."}]},{"i":252,"j":269,"loc":"Book I, lemmas: \"a certain region of Egypt, called Delta\" to \"you Greeks are always children\"","title":"Saïs, Neith, and the Greeks as children","summary":"Proclus follows the narrative's descent from Egypt to the Delta, the Saïtic province and Saïs, and reads it as the procession from comprehensive to partial causes. Egypt images the invisible order, the Delta the triangle that is the principle of the elements, the Nile the zodiac, and Saïs a link to the Bear. Neith, called Athena by Greeks, is the tutelar Goddess of both Saïs and Athens, and her inscription about the unopened garment is cited. Solon, honoured by the priests, recites Greek antiquities such as Phoroneus, Niobe and Deucalion; the old priest calls the Greeks always children, which Proclus explains by destructions and lack of records, and defends the value of such history.","ideas":["Geography read as procession from total to partial causes","One Goddess presides over Saïs and Athens in different degrees","Egyptian priests as images of the stable, older fabrication","Records of recurring periods aid recollection of wholes"],"terms":[{"t":"Neith","d":"The Egyptian name of the Goddess of Saïs, identified with Athena or Minerva."},{"t":"apocatastasis","d":"The restoration of things to a former configuration at the completion of a period."}]},{"i":270,"j":279,"loc":"Book I, lemmas: \"all your souls are juvenile\" to \"destructions…from fire and water\"","title":"Juvenile souls and the great destructions","summary":"The priest says Greek souls are juvenile, lacking ancient opinion and hoary discipline. Proclus reads youth and hoariness as partial versus stable, total knowledge, fitting different orders of Gods. He argues that wholes always remain in their natural state while parts are sometimes natural and sometimes preternatural, so that species endure while individuals perish, depending on climate, celestial configuration and presiding Gods. He explains why fire and water cause the greatest destructions: they act on us without first being affected themselves, whereas air and earth, akin to us, harm us only through their own distempers.","ideas":["Hoariness and juvenility as modes of divine knowledge","Wholes stay natural; parts vary between natural and preternatural","Species persist through periodic corruption of parts","Why deluges and conflagrations are the greatest destructions"],"terms":[{"t":"preternatural","d":"Contrary to nature, a condition parts can fall into while wholes cannot."}]},{"i":280,"j":298,"loc":"Book I, lemmas: \"Phæton the offspring of the Sun\" to \"a destruction…from the devastations of fire\"","title":"The fable of Phæton explained","summary":"Proclus reads the Phæton story historically, physically and philosophically. Physically, he sets aside the comet theory reported by Porphyry in favour of Domninus' account of a dry exhalation kindled by the Sun, with Jupiter's thunder as its extinction and the Heliades' tears as the rain that follows. Philosophically, souls are sown under various leading Gods and take on their character; Phæton is a solar soul that fell into generation wearing fiery vestments and burned places as it descended, while sister solar souls watched over it. On the priest's explanation, the mutation of the heavenly bodies means earthly things becoming out of measure with celestial influences or changing configurations, and widespread destructions take long periods because many causes must coincide.","ideas":["Phæton as a kindled exhalation, after Domninus","Souls take the peculiarity of their presiding Gods","Descending souls carry elemental vestments","Large destructions need many causes and long periods"],"terms":[{"t":"vehicle (ochēma)","d":"The subtle body of a soul, which shares the character of its leading God."},{"t":"genesiurgic","d":"Belonging to or producing generation, the sublunary realm of becoming."}]},{"i":299,"j":317,"loc":"Book I, lemmas: \"those who…dwell on mountains\" to \"you…commit only recent transactions to writing\"","title":"Mountains, the Nile, and Egyptian records","summary":"Proclus reports Porphyry's moral reading of fire and flood as anger and desire in the soul, and Iamblichus' physical reading of why highlanders die in fires and lowlanders in floods. The Nile, saviour of Egypt, images divine providence, and the Gods are named as the efficient cause of purifications by water and fire. He reviews theories of the Nile's rise (from below, from rains, Eratosthenes, Theophrastus) and notes that even Egypt could fail in infinite time. Domninus explains that men always survive in temperate climates, which Orpheus also says. The temple records that preserve all great and strange events are compared to Pythagorean recollection of past lives and to stable productive powers, while Greek writing and contrivances image the newer fabrication.","ideas":["Ethical (Porphyry) and physical (Iamblichus) readings of the destructions","Destructions as purifications sent by the Gods","Debate over the cause of the Nile's flooding","Temple records image the permanent powers of the cosmos"],"terms":[{"t":"Domninus","d":"Proclus' fellow student, whose interpretations he cites with approval."}]},{"i":318,"j":334,"loc":"Book I, lemmas: \"a celestial effluxion rushes on them\" to \"a most excellent city of Athenians\"","title":"Many deluges, ancient Athens, and cosmic opposition","summary":"The priest calls periodic deluges a celestial effluxion like a disease, evil to parts but good for the whole, and says the illiterate survivors leave the Greeks always young again, like souls forgetting intelligibles. He calls Solon's antiquities puerile fables, notes that there were many deluges before one, and tells of an excellent earlier Athenian race from whom Solon descends through a small seed. Proclus reads the most warlike and best-governed city as imitating its philosophic and warlike Goddess. He weighs arguments for and against interpreting the story, judges it both history and symbol, and sets out how opposition divides the universe at every level of the divine orders, from bound and infinity down to heaven and generation.","ideas":["Periodic destructions follow celestial circulations","Forgetting by survivors images souls forgetting intelligibles","The story is both true history and symbol of cosmic contrariety","Opposition runs from bound and infinity down through every order"],"terms":[{"t":"bound and infinity","d":"The first two principles from which all opposition in the universe derives."}]},{"i":335,"j":357,"loc":"Book I, lemmas: \"Solon…entreated the priests\" to \"Who is allotted the guardianship…\"","title":"The priest's generosity and divine allotments","summary":"Solon's wonder and request, and the priest's ungrudging reply for Solon, the city and the Goddess, are read as lower natures adhering to higher ones and as the Demiurgus' lack of envy. Proclus explains Minerva's veil as the last image of cosmic contrariety, preceded by the war in the universe and by the veil in her own intellect. He then asks what divine allotments are: the cosmos is divided by demiurgic numbers, with angelic and dæmonic allotments under divine ones. Sublunary allotments are perpetual because they rest on unchanging things such as cosmic interval, the vehicles of divine souls and the wholes of the elements; places only become fit or unfit at times to receive the Gods, so Minerva can hold both Athens and Saïs.","ideas":["Wonder joins the one who wonders to its object","The veil of Minerva images the ordered war of the universe","Allotments divided by demiurgic numbers","Gods' allotments are perpetual; places' aptitude to receive them varies"],"terms":[{"t":"allotment (lēxis)","d":"The region or domain assigned to a God's providence and government."},{"t":"telestic art","d":"Sacred rites by which places or statues are made fit to receive divine presence."}]},{"i":358,"j":362,"loc":"Book I, lemma: \"receiving the seed of your race from Vulcan and the Earth\"","title":"Vulcan, Earth, and the thousand years","summary":"Proclus explains Vulcan as the demiurgic God who fabricates all sensible bodies; his brass, bellows, lameness and fall from Olympus are symbols of this work. Earth stands for every material cause, so the Athenians arising from Vulcan's seed and Earth means productive powers joined to matter, and Vulcan's love for Minerva means imitating her intellect in making sensibles. The thousand years by which Athens precedes Saïs signify the higher rank of Athenian souls, a thousand being a cubic number. He ends with Iamblichus' doubt about how Gods can receive places at definite times and his answer: allotments are perpetual, while participation varies, being essential or by habitude.","ideas":["Vulcan as maker of the whole corporeal world","Earth as the material cause of generation","A thousand years mark a superior Minervan life","Allotments essential versus by habitude"],"terms":[{"t":"Vulcan (Hephaestus)","d":"For Proclus, the God who fabricates and adorns all bodies and the visible seats of the Gods."}]},{"i":363,"j":372,"loc":"Book I, on Tim. 23e–24a","title":"Nine thousand years and the ancient laws","summary":"Proclus comments on the priest's figures of nine thousand years for Athens and eight thousand for Saïs, reading them through number: eight thousand as a cube on a cube, nine thousand as a square on a cube, the simpler figure signifying the better life. He reports Porphyry's reading of the numbers as periods of souls tied to Vulcan and the lunar sphere, and Iamblichus' rejection of it in favour of measures of participation in Minerva. On the priest's promise to tell the laws briefly, he takes the brevity as an image of intellect gathering many powers into one. The laws of the ancient Athenians are called paradigms because images come first in our knowledge, though archetypes come first by nature.","ideas":["The numbers 8000 and 9000 signify grades of life and of participation in Minerva","Porphyry's psychological reading is set against Iamblichus' theological one","Socrates' polity is more universal; the priest's laws are more partial, like fate beside providence","Images can be called paradigms because we know the prior natures through them"],"terms":[{"t":"chiliad","d":"A thousand, the unit by which the priest, and by Porphyry's account the dæmons, measure lives and time."},{"t":"middle fabrication","d":"The second, more divided stage of the world's making, to which Proclus refers the Atlantic story."}]},{"i":373,"j":386,"loc":"Book I, on Tim. 24a–b","title":"The separated classes and Minerva's armour","summary":"The priest lists six separate classes: priests, soldiers, artisans, shepherds, hunters and farmers. Proclus argues that these, double the three classes of Socrates' polity, show the Egyptian order to be more partial, and that with the wisdom-loving class they make a Minerval heptad. He reports Porphyry's mapping of the classes onto archangels, descending souls and dæmons, Iamblichus' sharp rejection of it, and Iamblichus' own scheme, then gives his teacher's pairing of the classes into three duads with divine paradigms. Each class keeping to its own art produces both accuracy and mutual dependence among citizens. The shield and spear are read, after Iamblichus, as the powers by which the divine stays impassive and cuts away the material.","ideas":["Six classes plus the contemplative make seven, a number proper to Minerva","Iamblichus calls Porphyry's archangels and soul-hunters unplatonic and barbaric","Unmixed classes yield sameness with difference, separation with union","The shield stands for impassivity, the spear for action that cuts away matter"],"terms":[{"t":"anagogic Gods","d":"Gods that lead upward, in whom the priestly class has its paradigm."},{"t":"heptad","d":"The number seven, called motherless and Minerval because it is produced from the monad alone."}]},{"i":387,"j":401,"loc":"Book I, on Tim. 24b–d","title":"Prudence, medicine, and the choice of Attica","summary":"Proclus takes the laws' care for prudence, divination and medicine as the wisdom-loving side of Minerva, and distinguishes divine, dæmonic and human forms of both divination and medicine. He sides with Iamblichus against Porphyry: prudence is not artistic skill, which is Vulcan's gift, and Æsculapius belongs to the sun, not the moon. On the goddess choosing the place for its good blend of seasons, he rejects literal climate (Panætius), Longinus' nameless local quality, and Origen's appeal to the heavens alone. He holds instead that the gods divide space so each portion receives souls suited to its presiding deity, with the Seasons guarding these portions and celestial periods making wise men more or less frequent.","ideas":["Divination and medicine exist at divine, dæmonic and human levels","Divine choice and divine allotment coincide and are eternal","The 'place' is the immovable interval, divided among the gods","Fertile and barren periods change how many wise men a place yields"],"terms":[{"t":"eukrasia (good temperature)","d":"The good blending of seasons, which Proclus reads as a region's fitness to receive wise souls."},{"t":"interval","d":"The incorporeal extension underlying bodies, which the world-soul marks with affinities to different gods."}]},{"i":402,"j":421,"loc":"Book I, on Tim. 24c–d","title":"Minerva, lover of war and of wisdom","summary":"Proclus explains why the goddess is called both philopolemic and philosophic. He rejects Porphyry's placing of Minerva in the moon and builds on Iamblichus: Minerva is one of the first intellectual unities in the Demiurgus, born from his head, the source of his immutability and separate wisdom. She is philosophic as containing her father's wisdom, and warlike as ruling over all contrariety; Orphic and Homeric verses, her titles and her role against the Giants support the reading. The ancient Athenians, surpassing all men in virtue as pupils of the gods, image the more divine parts of the universe that are always perfected through her.","ideas":["Minerva is an undefiled intellectual unity within the Demiurgus","Wisdom and war correspond to separate intellect and mastery over contraries","Her procession reaches Proserpine's order, the liberated Gods and the heavens","Love and Minerva are both philosophers, but at different ranks"],"terms":[{"t":"theonoē","d":"Divine intellection, the name Socrates in the Cratylus is said to give Minerva."},{"t":"Metis","d":"Wisdom as a name of the Demiurgus, through which he brings forth Minerva."}]},{"i":422,"j":433,"loc":"Book I, on Tim. 24e","title":"The Atlantic war as cosmic contrariety","summary":"The priest announces the city's greatest deed: subduing a vast power from the Atlantic sea. Proclus notes that Plato skips the usual Persian Wars of Panathenaic speeches and, rather than portray a war of the gods, presents a human war analogous to the Olympians against the Titans and Giants, and to Minerva's victory over Neptune. Those within the Pillars of Hercules stand for the better coordination of things, those outside for the worse, at every level from gods to bodies. The Atlantic sea stands for matter, and the two orders, though contrary, derive from one principle and are harmonized into one world.","ideas":["Athens at the centre repels barbarians from east (Persia) and west (Atlantis)","Atlas the Titan links the Atlantics to the Titanic order","The two coordinations follow monad and duad, bound and infinity","Iamblichus' purely literal reading is respectfully set aside"],"terms":[{"t":"coordination (systoichia)","d":"One of the two parallel series of things, better and worse, running from the gods down to bodies."},{"t":"sea of dissimilitude","d":"Plato's image of matter, from the Politicus, here equated with the Atlantic sea."}]},{"i":434,"j":446,"loc":"Book I, on Tim. 24e–25a","title":"The Atlantic island, its islands and kings","summary":"Proclus cites Marcellus' Ethiopic History for islands in the outer sea preserving memory of Atlantis, so the account can be taken as history and as an image. He maps the island, the other islands and the opposite continent onto the first, middle and last genera of the worse coordination, with magnitude signifying distance from the one. The Pillars of Hercules mark the demiurgic division of the two parts of the cosmos, and the narrow harbour stands for the self-converging better order. Defending the island's size, he appeals to Plato's view that the earth is far larger than mathematicians reckon, then reads the ten kings, five pairs of twins sons of Neptune, through the decad, pentad and duad.","ideas":["Quantity increases as power decreases with distance from the one","Island, islands and continent are first, middle and last genera","Plato's earth is immense, with us living in a hollow","The kings' dominion beyond the Pillars shows lower parts of the better serving the worse"],"terms":[{"t":"Pillars of Hercules","d":"Read as the demiurgic boundary keeping the two coordinations distinct."},{"t":"decad","d":"Ten, which by the Pythagoreans comprehends all opposites, hence ten kings."}]},{"i":447,"j":457,"loc":"Book I, on Tim. 25b–d","title":"Athens' victory and the sinking of Atlantis","summary":"The Atlantic power, gathered into one, tries to enslave the lands within the strait and is defeated by Athens through virtue and strength. Proclus reads the outcome as the better coordination mastering the worse: the Athenians are the first genera, the unenslaved Greeks the middle, and those freed the last, with souls passing at times under the Titanic and at times under the Olympian order. He then defends the earthquake and flood physically, citing Aristotle and the destruction of Helice, and explains the shoal mud. Allegorically, the Athenians going under earth and Atlantis vanishing in the sea imitate the Orphic casting into Tartarus, Neptunian powers carrying both orders to the extremities of the world.","ideas":["Virtue belongs to bound, mere power to infinity","Higher causes act before, with and after their effects","Earthquake and deluge are natural, as with Helice and Bouras","Earth signifies stability; sea signifies flux and formlessness"],"terms":[{"t":"Titanic and Olympian orders","d":"The divided and the unifying divine ranks whose conflict the Atlantic war images."},{"t":"Tartarus","d":"The lowest region, to which the Titanic order is driven at the end of the fabrication."}]},{"i":458,"j":472,"loc":"Book I, on Tim. 25e–26d","title":"Critias' recollection and the memory of childhood","summary":"Critias says he noticed how Socrates' polity matched Solon's story, held back until he had recalled it fully overnight, and now will show the ideal citizens to be the ancient Athenians. Proclus summarizes the cosmic reading of the war, interprets the exchange of benefits between Egypt and Athens and the role of fortune, and takes Critias' delay as an image of the Demiurgus first conceiving his productions within himself. Explaining why children remember well, he gives Porphyry's reasons: untroubled imagination, reason mixed with fantasy, and things seeming greater and more moving. He adds Iamblichus' reading of the indelible letters as the never-failing fabrication.","ideas":["The story shows Socrates' polity is possible and admirable","Fortune is a power that gathers scattered causes","Strong delight or pain fixes impressions in memory","Critias gives first the whole of the war, then promises the parts"],"terms":[{"t":"phantasia (imagination)","d":"The image-forming power, which in children is more strongly marked by what they hear."}]},{"i":473,"j":484,"loc":"Book I, on Tim. 26e–27b","title":"The order of speakers and end of the preface","summary":"Socrates accepts the story as fitting the goddess's festival and as true history, and agrees to listen. Proclus explains good Fortune as guiding speech, then the plan: Timæus generates men from the world's origin, Socrates has educated them, Critias leads them to action, and Hermocrates will help with speeches. He answers why the Republic precedes the Timæus though nature puts cosmology first: an account in words alone comes first, and education must precede contemplation and then action. Timæus is most astronomical in contemplating causes above the heavens, and the invocation of the gods shows that physiology must hang from theology, as Porphyry and Iamblichus held against Longinus' dismissal of the preface.","ideas":["Timæus is both summit and middle, like the whole fabrication","The four speakers image paternal, stable, moving and converting causes","Man is a microcosm, so cosmology ends in man","True physiology begins from a divine cause, not airs and æthers"],"terms":[{"t":"physiologia","d":"The study of nature, which Proclus insists depends on theology."}]},{"i":485,"j":503,"loc":"Book II, on Tim. 27b–c","title":"The preface recapitulated; the nature of prayer","summary":"Book II opens by summing up the preface: the polity images the first fabrication and the heavens, the war the second and generation. Timæus prays before beginning, imitating the Demiurgus consulting Night in Orphic verse. Proclus reports Porphyry's case for prayer against those who deny gods or providence, then, following Iamblichus, sets out his own account: all things proceed from and return to the gods through symbols the Demiurgus planted in souls, and prayer uses them to convert the soul. Perfect prayer needs knowledge of the divine orders, likeness of life, contact, approach and finally union; he closes with prayer's causes, kinds and objects.","ideas":["All things abide in, proceed from and return to the gods","Prayer unites the one of the soul with the one of the gods","Faith, truth and love belong to the ascent","Prayers are demiurgic, cathartic or vivific; soul, body, externals in that rank"],"terms":[{"t":"synthēmata (symbols)","d":"Divine tokens sown in souls and things, through which they turn back to their gods."},{"t":"henōsis (union)","d":"The last and highest stage of prayer, where the soul's activity becomes one with the divine."}]},{"i":504,"j":512,"loc":"Book II, on Tim. 27c","title":"Invoking divinity before speaking of the universe","summary":"Timæus says even the slightly temperate call on divinity before any undertaking. Proclus explains that temperance here is the soul's turning to itself and to the gods, seeing that small things are great when suspended from providence; impulses, more than choices, need divine help. He meets Eurymachus' objection of an infinite regress of prayers, finding Porphyry's answer insufficient and holding that prayer carries its own good. On Timæus' 'in a certain respect' he prefers his teacher's view that it marks the instructional level of speech, and on 'whether generated or ungenerated' he reviews readings by Albinus and others, siding with Porphyry and Iamblichus.","ideas":["Physiology becomes a kind of theology when nature is seen as from the gods","Timæus keeps a mean between irony and boasting","Speech is demiurgic, scientific or instructional","Whether the world is generated is the first question of the whole inquiry"],"terms":[{"t":"sōphrosynē (temperance)","d":"Here an inspired turning of the soul toward the gods, not mere self-control."}]},{"i":513,"j":523,"loc":"Book II, on Tim. 27c–d","title":"Gods and goddesses, and the first division","summary":"Timæus invokes gods and goddesses; Proclus says male and female together comprise all divine orders, as heaven to earth. He asks why Timæus does not actually pray and answers that prayer is fulfilled in the will itself, and that Timæus carries out what he asks by conducting the argument well. His exhortation of the hearers and his 'it seems to me' show the Pythagorean manner and the three levels of intellect, dianoia and opinion. Proclus argues that distinguishing being from becoming is not a division of a genus, whole, word or accident, but a separate definition of each, made so that, as from geometrical hypotheses, the Demiurgus and paradigm can be located.","ideas":["Prayer's perfection lies in the will to turn to the gods","Timæus speaks enunciatively, unlike Socrates' dialectic","Being and becoming share no common genus","From 'generated' follow a cause, a Demiurgus and a paradigm"],"terms":[{"t":"dianoia","d":"Discursive reason, which produces sciences, between intellect and opinion."},{"t":"diaireteon","d":"Plato's 'must be distinguished', read by Proclus as 'must be defined apart'."}]},{"i":524,"j":536,"loc":"Book II, on Tim. 27d–28a (1 of 2)","title":"What 'that which always is' includes","summary":"On 'what is always being, without generation', Proclus asks whether the division covers all beings. He explains why Plato defines perpetual being without first proving it exists: Socrates' earlier arguments, the prayer itself, and the impossibility of infinite regress support it, and Plato later demonstrates it. Since both paradigm and Demiurgus count as perpetual being, he rejects Iamblichus' restriction of the term to the summit of the intelligible. Perpetual being covers every eternal nature from animal itself down to partial intellects, while the one being stands above eternity.","ideas":["Science proceeds from hypotheses, like geometry","The paradigm and the Demiurgus are both eternal beings","The one being is prior to eternity, and eternity prior to the eternal","Things exist by cause, by hyparxis or by participation"],"terms":[{"t":"aei on (perpetual being)","d":"That which always is, taken here as the whole eternal world prior to soul."},{"t":"hyparxis","d":"A thing's own proper mode of existence, contrasted with existence in its cause or by participation."}]},{"i":537,"j":551,"loc":"Book II, on Tim. 27d–28a (2 of 2)","title":"Becoming, soul as middle, and the principles","summary":"Proclus explains that 'without generation' marks perpetual being's purity from time, and that 'what is generated' means every bodily nature, which is always or at some time becoming and never truly is. Soul is the middle: last of eternal natures and first of generated ones, so the division both does and does not include all beings. He lists the principles of physiology Plato lays down like a geometer's definitions, compares Aristotle's hypotheses about the fifth body, and explains why Plato says 'always' rather than 'itself' or 'that which is'. Perpetual being is not generated by any cause but exists according to causes.","ideas":["Soul is ungenerated and generated, eternal and not eternal","The physicist assumes, but does not prove, his principles","'Itself', 'that which is' and 'ever' come from paradigm, one being and eternity","An eternal 'ever' is all at once; a temporal 'ever' is unending extension"],"terms":[{"t":"genesis (becoming)","d":"What is always coming to be and has its existence suspended from another."},{"t":"aiōn (eternity)","d":"The source of perpetuity to intelligibles, distinct from the infinity of time."}]},{"i":552,"j":561,"loc":"Book II, on Tim. 28a (1 of 4)","title":"Intelligence together with reason","summary":"Timæus says being is grasped by intelligence with reason, becoming by opinion with irrational sense. Proclus answers critics who complain the definitions lack a genus and define by knowledge: being has no genus, and definitions useful for demonstration must be known to us. He distinguishes six grades of intelligence, from the intelligible itself to imagination, and argues that the intelligence meant is that of a partial intellect, joined by the summit of our reason. Reason in this sense is the most unified part of the soul, which sees being in company with intellect, circling it as a centre.","ideas":["Plato uses affirmation for being, negation for becoming","Intelligence has six grades, the imagination being the lowest","A partial intellect stands just above our soul and illuminates it","Reason sees the intelligible transitively, intellect without transition"],"terms":[{"t":"noēsis (intelligence)","d":"Intellectual intuition; here that of a partial intellect, shared by reason when purified."},{"t":"logos (reason)","d":"Here the highest, most unified part of the rational soul, not speech or discursive account."}]},{"i":562,"j":569,"loc":"Book II, on Tim. 28a (2 of 4)","title":"Opinion, sense, and Aristotle's objection","summary":"Proclus defines opinion as the lowest rational power, holding the reasons of sensibles and knowing that a thing is, without its cause. Sense knows only its own affections; the example of an apple shows a higher power must judge that the whole is an apple, and sense keeps seeing the sun a foot wide whatever reason says. Opinion with sense therefore grasps what is generated. Against Aristotle's objection that the heaven is perceived by sense yet ungenerated, Proclus argues that a body can neither produce nor hold itself together, so a finite body is generated in its own nature, and the everlasting differs from the eternal.","ideas":["The ranks: intelligence, reason, dianoia, opinion, sense, sense-organ","Sense is wholly irrational and disobeys reason","Opinion with sense knows becoming, as reason with intellect knows being","Heaven is perpetual through all time, not eternal"],"terms":[{"t":"doxa (opinion)","d":"Knowledge of the essences of sensibles through inner reasons, without knowledge of causes."},{"t":"aisthēsis (sense)","d":"Irrational awareness of affections produced in the body by sensibles."}]},{"i":570,"j":578,"loc":"Book II, on Tim. 28a (3 of 4)","title":"The criteria of truth, middles and extremes","summary":"From these definitions Proclus draws Plato's view of the criteria: intellect for intelligibles, dianoia, opinion and sense for their objects, all used by a single reason. He then restates the scheme: what is eternal in essence, power and activity is perpetual being; what has all these in time is simply generated; soul and time are intermediates; matter and the one fall below and above both. Following Porphyry, he notes Plato defines only the extremes. By altering the definitions, he derives how the middle is known (reason and opinion), how matter is known (spurious reason, insensible sense), and how the one is known.","ideas":["Intelligence is the light of reason, not its instrument (against Severus)","Perpetual being reaches down to partial intellects of dæmons and souls","Affirmations and negations separate the extremes from the middle","Matter and the one are each known by a spurious knowledge"],"terms":[{"t":"kritērion (criterion)","d":"The power by which truth is judged, distributed by Plato according to its objects."},{"t":"spurious reasoning","d":"The bastard cognition by which matter is grasped, as later in the Timæus."}]},{"i":579,"j":585,"loc":"Book II, on Tim. 28a (4 of 4)","title":"Whatever is generated has a cause","summary":"Timæus lays down that everything generated is generated by a cause. Proclus treats this as a geometer's axiom and casts it, with Iamblichus, as a categorical syllogism in which 'impossible without a cause' is the clearer middle term. What is generated cannot preserve or produce itself, so it needs a maker; the 'cause' meant is the efficient, demiurgic one, and 'a certain' cause because different makers produce different things. He enumerates Aristotelian and Platonic kinds of cause, rejects the Epicureans' chance and the Peripatetics' causeless chance, and relates the axiom to the Philebus' cause of mixture.","ideas":["'Necessary' joins effect to cause; 'impossible' views it apart from cause","Many demiurgic causes imply one cause before them","A golden chain of causes rules the universe under one providence","Demiurgus, form, matter, generated thing answer to one, bound, infinite, mixture"],"terms":[{"t":"efficient (demiurgic) cause","d":"The making cause, signalled by Plato's 'by which'."},{"t":"axiōma (axiom)","d":"A common conception laid down before demonstration, as in geometry."}]},{"i":586,"j":598,"loc":"Book II, on Tim. 28a–b","title":"The eternal paradigm and beauty","summary":"Timæus says a maker looking to an eternal paradigm produces something beautiful, while a generated paradigm yields nothing beautiful. Proclus builds a chain: the world is generated, so it has a maker, so it has a paradigm; and since it is beautiful, its paradigm is eternal. Beauty comes from the paradigm and likeness from the maker, as Phidias' Zeus shows. Against Epicureans, Stoics and Peripatetics he argues for a separate Demiurgus containing paradigms, partly from Aristotle's own premise that a finite body has no infinite power. He then distinguishes ranks of demiurgic causes and their paradigms, and agrees with Porphyry that 'always' qualifies the paradigm, not the Demiurgus' gaze.","ideas":["Paradigms are of three kinds, eternal or generated, of eternal or generated things","Works of art are neither beautiful nor ugly, lacking intelligible paradigms","The paradigm gives form, the Demiurgus order, the good union","The same maker can look to either paradigm, as soul to intellect or becoming"],"terms":[{"t":"paradeigma (paradigm)","d":"The model a maker looks to; for the world, the intelligible animal itself."},{"t":"Demiurgus","d":"The maker of the world, held by Proclus to be separate from matter and a true efficient cause."}]},{"i":599,"j":610,"loc":"Book II, on Tim. 28b (1 of 2)","title":"Naming the universe; the first question","summary":"Timæus' last axiom names the universe 'heaven' or 'world' or whatever it may wish to be called. Proclus explains the ambiguity of the names among the ancients, takes 'heaven' as the universe turning back to its source and 'world' as proceeding from it, and compares the world to a consecrated statue with hidden divine signs. The words 'whatever other name' hint at an ineffable divine name known only to the gods, as Homer distinguishes divine and human names. Then the first question is posed: whether the world always was or came to be from some principle, which Proclus, against Porphyry, reads as applying to every inquiry.","ideas":["Divine names reveal the whole essence; human names touch it partially","Abiding, procession and return correspond to the three names","Plato asks not 'eternal or generated' but 'always or from some principle'","The world is generated in body but in another respect ungenerated"],"terms":[{"t":"ouranos and kosmos","d":"Heaven and world, names of the universe as turning to and proceeding from the intelligible."}]},{"i":611,"j":621,"loc":"Book II, on Tim. 28b (2 of 2)","title":"In what sense the world is generated","summary":"Proclus surveys interpretations: Plutarch and Atticus take generation as temporal, after a disorderly motion; Crantor's followers as being produced by another; Plotinus, Porphyry and Iamblichus as being composite. He accepts both of the last two and adds that whatever lacks its whole essence or activity all at once is generated. The world, unable to hold itself together, always receives its existence anew from its cause, enduring through all of time rather than in eternity. 'Generated' has many senses, and the world has 'a certain' principle of generation, the final cause, not a temporal beginning.","ideas":["Between eternal things and things of limited duration is the everlasting","No finite body can receive eternity's infinite power","What has a temporal beginning has all senses of generation; the world has not","The world is always beginning and always complete"],"terms":[{"t":"genētos (generated)","d":"Said of what has a temporal start, comes from a cause, is composite, or could come to be."},{"t":"final principle","d":"The good for whose sake the world comes to be, its most proper principle."}]},{"i":622,"j":634,"loc":"Book II, on Tim. 28b–c (1 of 2)","title":"'It was generated': against Atticus","summary":"Timæus answers that the world was generated, since it is visible, tangible and bodily, hence sensible and grasped by opinion. Proclus praises the method: conclusion first, like intellect, then a demonstration using the definition as middle term. Against Atticus, who holds the disorderly motion ungenerated but the world generated in time, he argues both were visible, so both must be generated or neither. He then argues Plato does not deny the world's perpetuity: time came to be with heaven, the world-soul is always, the world is indestructible, the Laws speak of time infinite in the past, and an eternal Demiurgus cannot begin to make at some moment.","ideas":["Visible and tangible mark heaven and earth, the world's extremes","There cannot be a disorderly and an orderly time","Whatever is incorruptible is not generated in time","An idle-then-active maker would be imperfect and in time"],"terms":[{"t":"Atticus","d":"Platonist who read the Timæus as describing a creation of the world in time."}]},{"i":635,"j":645,"loc":"Book II, on Tim. 28b–c (2 of 2)","title":"Everlasting generation, Plato and Aristotle","summary":"Proclus rejects Severus' view that only the present cosmic revolution was generated, and the view that generation is only a teaching device. The world is generated as always coming to be and always having come to be, like light from the sun; it is called generated from its body, as Socrates is called mortal, though as ensouled and intelligent it is a god. Plato's silence about destruction shows reverence: a body cannot hold itself together, so it is destructible in itself but receives endless existence from its unmoved cause. Plato and Aristotle agree on the world's perpetuity but differ on its maker, and Plato ties generation and corruption together, so his 'generation' is meant differently from its surface sense.","ideas":["Fabled reversals of the cosmos (Politicus) do not belong in physics","The world receives infinite power by flowing participation, never all at once","Aristotle assigns to intellect, heavens and motion what Plato assigns to the one, Demiurgus and essence","Generated and corruptible go together, so temporal generation is ruled out"],"terms":[{"t":"always becoming","d":"The world's mode of being: perpetually receiving existence from its cause through all time."},{"t":"infinite power","d":"Power no finite body has of itself, which the world receives continually from its maker."}]},{"i":646,"j":656,"loc":"Tim. 28a–c (lemmas: 'generated by a certain cause'; 'difficult to discover the maker and father')","title":"The world's cause and finding its father","summary":"Proclus explains the second hypothesis: whatever is generated is imperfect and cannot perfect or compose itself, so the generated world must have a cause outside itself. Plato says 'a certain cause' because the Demiurgus is one cause but not the first cause of all, which is simply God. On the next lemma Proclus distinguishes father (giver of being and union) from maker (giver of form and order), rejecting Porphyry's account of the difference. Discovering the Demiurgus is hard because it requires surveying all his works and then rising to an intellectual union with him beyond opinion and science. He cannot be spoken of to all, both because of Pythagorean reserve and because what is grasped in silent intellection cannot be put into words.","ideas":["The generated, being imperfect, is perfected and composed only by another","'A certain cause' marks a single but not the first cause","Father and maker name distinct aspects of one productive god","Finding the father is intellectual union, not syllogistic knowledge"],"terms":[{"t":"Demiurgus (Demiurge)","d":"The divine craftsman-intellect that produces and orders the universe."},{"t":"hypothesis","d":"One of the first principles Timaeus lays down, from which the account of the world is demonstrated."}]},{"i":657,"j":667,"loc":"Tim. 28c (commentary on the Demiurgus)","title":"Earlier interpreters on who the Demiurgus is","summary":"Proclus reviews and criticizes earlier views on the Demiurgus's rank. Numenius splits father and maker into two gods, and Harpocration contradicts himself with shifting triads of gods. Atticus wrongly identifies the Demiurgus with the Good, Plotinus rightly posits a twofold Demiurgus, and Amelius makes three demiurgic intellects. Porphyry makes the Demiurgus a supermundane soul, which Proclus says Plato never does. Iamblichus seems to make the whole intelligible world the Demiurgus, though elsewhere he places him more exactly in the intellectual hebdomad. Theodorus places three Demiurgi at the end of the intelligible-intellectual gods; Proclus answers that a demiurgic monad must come before any triad.","ideas":["Plato names one Demiurgus by both 'father' and 'maker'","The Demiurgus is good but not the Good","Every divine order begins from a monad, not a triad","Soul cannot produce what ranks above soul"],"terms":[{"t":"monad","d":"The single principle that heads and precedes the multitude of a divine order."}]},{"i":668,"j":689,"loc":"Tim. 28c (Syrianus's view of the Demiurgus)","title":"Syrianus: the Demiurgus is Jupiter","summary":"Proclus sets out the view of his teacher Syrianus: the one Demiurgus stands at the boundary of the intellectual gods, the third of the intellectual fathers, heading a demiurgic triad. He is called intellect and 'best of causes' and looks to the intelligible paradigm, and his chief work is to produce intellect. Proclus grades 'father', 'father and maker', 'maker and father' and 'maker', ranking Phanes as more father and Jupiter as more maker. Citing Orphic verses on Jupiter swallowing Phanes and holding all things, and passages in the Cratylus, Gorgias, Politicus and Protagoras, he identifies the Demiurgus with Jupiter. He concludes the Demiurgus is a fontal god, not one of the ruling gods, since he is the summit of the demiurgic series.","ideas":["The Demiurgus is the third intellectual father","Orpheus and Plato both celebrate Jupiter as maker of the whole","He makes intellect alone, soul with the Crater, body with necessity","As 'best of causes' he is fontal rather than ruling"],"terms":[{"t":"fontal (pēgaios)","d":"Belonging to the highest, source-level rank of a divine series, above the ruling gods."},{"t":"Crater","d":"The mixing bowl; here a power, identified with Juno, with which the Demiurgus generates soul."}]},{"i":690,"j":704,"loc":"Tim. 28c–29a (lemma: 'according to which of the paradigms')","title":"That there is a paradigm, and where","summary":"Timaeus now asks whether the world was made after an eternal or a generated paradigm. Proclus argues that any orderly maker must hold the measure of what it makes, so a knowing Demiurgus implies a paradigm. He reports the views of Iamblichus, Porphyry, Theodorus, Plotinus and Longinus, then gives Syrianus's answer: the paradigm is intelligibly prior to the Demiurgus and intellectually within him. Orpheus's Protogonus (Phanes), swallowed by Jupiter, is Plato's 'animal itself'. Proclus solves the doubt about what 'generated' paradigm could exist before the world: it is the confused, disorderly nature, which was itself generated, against Atticus and Plutarch.","ideas":["Every orderly maker holds the form of its product","The paradigm is prior to the Demiurgus and also within him","Animal itself corresponds to the Orphic Phanes","The pre-cosmic disorderly nature counts as 'generated', not unbegotten"],"terms":[{"t":"paradigm","d":"The model or pattern according to which the Demiurgus makes the world."},{"t":"animal itself (autozōon)","d":"The intelligible living being that serves as the paradigm of the universe."}]},{"i":705,"j":722,"loc":"Tim. 29a–b (lemmas: 'if this world is beautiful'; 'manifest to every one'; 'image of a certain thing')","title":"Beautiful world, best cause, eternal model","summary":"Proclus sets out Plato's reasoning: what is made after an eternal paradigm is beautiful, the world is plainly beautiful, so it was made after an eternal paradigm. The alternative, that the Demiurgus is not good, is left unsaid, out of reverence. 'Most beautiful' and 'best' show that the best cause looked to the first and most eternal paradigm. Proclus answers critics who call the world a cave or the Demiurgus's work sport: both hold only in comparison with the intelligible. On 'image', he adds that the world is not only made after the paradigm but truly mastered by form. The paradigm makes by its being and the Demiurgus by his activity, each being present in the other.","ideas":["The world's beauty proves an eternal paradigm","The most beautiful work implies the best maker and the highest model","Sensible, intellectual and intelligible universes are image, cause and paradigm","The paradigm makes by being, the Demiurgus by energy"],"terms":[{"t":"eikōn (image)","d":"A likeness that truly resembles and is mastered by its paradigm."}]},{"i":723,"j":743,"loc":"Tim. 29b–d (lemmas: 'begin from a principle according to nature'; words allied to things; 'as essence to generation, truth to faith')","title":"Starting points and two kinds of discourse","summary":"Proclus reviews readings of the maxim that beginning from a natural principle is the greatest task, and holds it applies to everything: the final cause, hypotheses and definitions are the proper starting points. Timaeus then lays down that words resemble the things they explain. Being, knowledge and words come in matching pairs: being and generation, intelligence and opinion, stable and probable discourse. Words about the eternal paradigm should be firm and irrefutable 'as far as possible', since scientific discourse falls short of intellect. Words about the image have only probability, and the faith meant here is rational but mixed with sense. Proclus ends by explaining why scientific language about the Demiurgus is still possible though he is hard to discover.","ideas":["Discourse must match the nature of its object","Each lower kind of knowledge is corrected by the one above it","Physics yields probable accounts because sensibles are unstable and remote","Truth exists at several levels: unitary, intelligible, psychical"],"terms":[{"t":"eikōs logos (probable account)","d":"The likely account suited to things that are images of being."},{"t":"pistis (faith, belief)","d":"Here a rational but sense-dependent belief about the generated world."}]},{"i":744,"j":757,"loc":"Tim. 29c–d (lemmas: 'you must not wonder, O Socrates'; 'calling to mind... the human nature'; Socrates' reply)","title":"Human limits and the accepted preface","summary":"Timaeus warns that his account of the gods and the generation of the universe cannot be wholly exact. Proclus explains that even talk of eternal things divides what is indivisible, and that astronomical claims are exact only as claims about mathematical, not sensible, objects. Physical accounts lack precision because their objects are material and because human knowing depends on sense. Against Porphyry, he holds that the gods know changing things unchangingly, since the mode of knowledge follows the knower. He praises Plato's modesty over Heraclitus, Empedocles and the Stoics. Socrates' praise of the 'preface' shows the right disposition of a hearer, and 'melody' (nomos) points to the harmonious order of the world.","ideas":["Inexactness comes from the objects and from human weakness","Knowledge varies with the knower, not the thing known","Astronomers save the phenomena by different hypotheses","The preface contains the whole plan of the account"],"terms":[{"t":"eikotologia (speaking from probability)","d":"The mode of discourse proper to physics, as opposed to strict science."},{"t":"nomos (melody, law)","d":"A musical strain; Proclus links it to the fixed, lawful order of the cosmos."}]},{"i":758,"j":780,"loc":"Tim. 29d–e (lemmas: 'on account of what cause'; 'He was good, and in the good no envy')","title":"The final cause: the Demiurgus was good","summary":"Timaeus turns to the final cause, which Proclus calls the most venerable of causes, since all others depend on it. He lists the prepositional names of the causes and rejects readings of 'generation' and 'this universe' as the sublunary realm or matter; following Syrianus, generation is the forming of bodies on the way to the whole. Proclus denies that the Demiurgus is the Good itself: he is good by participation, and goodness is what makes any god a god. Goodness is ranked above paradigm and intellect, and he gives Amelius's three Demiurgi a sense as aspects of the one Demiurgus. 'Was' marks his super-eternal nature, and freedom from envy follows because a god lacks no good.","ideas":["The final cause is prior to paradigm and maker","Goodness is the very hyparxis of the gods","The Demiurgus is good, not the Good","Envy is a lack of good and cannot touch the primarily good"],"terms":[{"t":"hyparxis (existence, subsistence)","d":"The highest, unitary mode of a god's being, identified here with goodness."},{"t":"phthonos (envy)","d":"Grudging, defined here as the lack of good, absent from the divine."}]},{"i":781,"j":796,"loc":"Tim. 29e–30a (lemmas: 'willing all things similar to himself'; 'most proper principle'; 'willing all things good')","title":"Divine will, providence and eternal making","summary":"From the Demiurgus's freedom from envy Proclus derives his will that all things become like him, and so a providence reaching to everything. Because the maker is always good, willing and able, he always produces; the world is therefore perpetual, always coming to be rather than being. Timaeus calls this the 'most proper principle', and Proclus explains why it must be received from prudent men. He reads the next lemma as moving from goodness to will to providence, sets these out as a triad, and matches it to analogous triads in the intelligible, intellect and soul. Against the claim that a good maker would stop at gods, he argues that goodness must be fruitful, so procession runs unbroken down to matter.","ideas":["Goodness, will and providence follow one another","A maker who is always good always makes, so the world is perpetual","The final cause is 'most principal' among causes","A good first cause must produce down to the last things"],"terms":[{"t":"pronoia (providence)","d":"The divine activity that gives good to all things and turns them back to the good."}]},{"i":797,"j":808,"loc":"Tim. 30a (commentary: 'that as much as possible nothing should be evil')","title":"How evil exists under a good god","summary":"Proclus takes up the dilemma that if god willed evil he is not good, and if not, something exists against his will. Following Syrianus, he answers that nothing is evil to god or to the whole; evil exists only for parts, and only mixed with good. He cites the Politicus, Republic and Theaetetus to show that Plato gives evil partial, indefinite causes rather than divine ones. Corruption serves the whole, and wrong choices bring their own just punishment, so even these are good in a certain respect. Self-moving partial souls are needed to keep the chain of beings continuous. Evil lies not in essences or powers but in a lack of proportion among activities, and has only a derived, shadowy existence.","ideas":["To the whole nothing is evil; evil exists for parts","Evil comes from partial causes, not from god","A depraved choice is its own punishment","Evil is a privation of symmetry with only a parasitic existence"],"terms":[{"t":"parypostasis","d":"A derived, parasitic quasi-existence, the only kind of being Proclus grants to evil."}]},{"i":809,"j":825,"loc":"Tim. 30a (lemma: 'receiving every thing that was visible... from disorder into order')","title":"Disorderly motion and the origin of matter","summary":"Plutarch and Atticus read the lemma literally, as a beginning in time with matter moved by a maleficent soul; Porphyry and Iamblichus reject this as putting the imperfect before the perfect. Proclus holds that Plato analyses in thought what always coexists, separating bodily formation from ordering. He argues that matter is not an unbegotten second principle: through the Philebus's bound and infinity, Orphic ether and chaos, and Hermes, it derives from the first principles. Different causes give different gifts: the Good gives matter, the paradigm gives form, the Demiurgus gives order. He then glosses 'visible', 'not at rest', 'led into order' and 'conceiving' (goodness, power, knowledge).","ideas":["Pre-cosmic disorder is an analytical abstraction, not a past state","Matter comes from the first infinity, not from itself","Good, paradigm and Demiurgus contribute matter, form and order","The Demiurgus contains order itself and makes by his being"],"terms":[{"t":"hulē (matter)","d":"The formless last infinity, receptacle of forms, derived ultimately from the first principles."},{"t":"peras and apeiria (bound and infinity)","d":"The two principles after the One from which all things, including matter, derive."}]},{"i":826,"j":845,"loc":"Tim. 30a (Porphyry against Atticus; lemma: 'not lawful for the best to make anything but the most beautiful')","title":"Against two principles; Themis and the best","summary":"Proclus likens the pre-cosmic disorder to the theologians' Titan wars, then reports Porphyry's case against Atticus under several heads. Two unbegotten principles, God and matter, could not differ without a further cause, leading to infinite regress or chance. A principle defined by ordering would depend on its products, and a god who sometimes fabricates would have an acquired power. Plato derives everything from one principle, as the Republic, Epistles and Philebus show. Disorder is introduced only to separate the generation of bodies from their ordering, and a divine intellect makes by its very being, as art, imagination and seed show on a small scale. On the next lemma, Themis, guardian of divine law, ensures the best maker produces the most beautiful work.","ideas":["Two unbegotten principles would leave their difference uncaused","Plato traces all things to one principle","True powers act by their mere presence","Themis binds the best cause to the most beautiful effect"],"terms":[{"t":"Themis","d":"Goddess of divine law; here the monad of cosmic order present with the Demiurgus."}]},{"i":846,"j":861,"loc":"Tim. 30b (lemmas: 'by a reasoning process'; 'intellect cannot accede without soul')","title":"Reasoning, intellect and the world-soul","summary":"Amelius sees three Demiurgi in willing, reasoning and receiving; Iamblichus rejects this. Proclus holds there is one Demiurgus who produces the whole by intellection and its parts by a distributing 'reasoning', which implies no doubt. The 'naturally visible' things he surveys are intelligibles, not sensibles, as Iamblichus says; there the causes of intelligent and unintelligent beings pre-exist. Goodness leads to beauty, beauty to intellect, intellect to soul, soul to ordered body. Soul is needed as the mean between impartible intellect and partible body, and the world's order shows it has both. Against Aristotle, Proclus posits both an imparticipable and a participated mundane intellect, and argues each implies the other.","ideas":["Logismos is the distributed production of parts, not deliberation","Intellectual beings are better than unintellectual ones","Soul is the bond between intellect and body","Aristotle omits imparticipable intellect and the intellectual soul"],"terms":[{"t":"logismos (reasoning process)","d":"Here the Demiurgus's distributing of the one into many parts, not a search for answers."},{"t":"imparticipable intellect","d":"Intellect that exists in itself, prior to the intellect shared by the world."}]},{"i":862,"j":883,"loc":"Tim. 30b–c (lemmas: 'intellect in soul, soul in body'; 'most beautiful according to nature'; 'an animal with soul and intellect through the providence of God')","title":"The world as ensouled, intelligent animal","summary":"Proclus argues the intellect placed in soul is an essential intellect, not a habit of soul. 'In' describes procession, by which causes are present throughout their effects; in conversion effects are in their causes. He matches this to the Orphic Hippa receiving Dionysus and to a Chaldean Oracle. The world is made most beautiful and best by participation in intelligible beauty and the Good. 'Probable' fits the world's mixed nature, 'truth' the divine providence. 'Animal', 'ensouled' and 'intelligent' come from distinct causes, and sympathy, perpetual motion and order show each. Against Chrysippus's immanent god, Plato sets three prior causes, and providence is the activity of goodness converting all to the good.","ideas":["Intellect in the world is a substance, not a habit of soul","'Animal' is wider than 'ensouled', 'ensouled' wider than 'intelligent'","Sympathy, motion and order show the world is animal, ensouled and intelligent","Providence is goodness in act"],"terms":[{"t":"proodos and epistrophē (procession and conversion)","d":"The outflow of effects from causes and their return to them."}]},{"i":884,"j":904,"loc":"Tim. 30c (lemmas: 'after the similitude of what animal'; 'not to any animal in the form of a part')","title":"Which intelligible animal is the model","summary":"Since the world is an animal, its paradigm must be an intelligible animal, and Proclus asks which, since animal proceeds through all orders of intellect. The paradigm is called animal because it is full of intelligible life; he places animal itself in the third intelligible triad, after being and eternity, and cites the Chaldean Oracles. 'Constituting artificer constituted' shows the maker makes by his being, always and completely. No partial animal can be the model, since parts are imperfect relative to the whole. Sun, moon and stars are beautiful but not most beautiful, being parts; he explains 'in the form of a part' and 'naturally'. He adds that the Good cannot be one intelligible form, nor the whole intelligible.","ideas":["The world is an animal because it is like an intelligible animal","Animal itself is the third intelligible triad","Parts are perfect but not all-perfect","The Good lies beyond the intelligible"],"terms":[{"t":"triad (intelligible)","d":"One of three orders of the intelligible gods: being, life (eternity), and animal itself."}]},{"i":905,"j":925,"loc":"Tim. 30c–31a (lemmas: 'most similar to that animal of which others are parts'; 'comprehends all intelligible animals'; 'one visible animal')","title":"Animal itself as Phanes; the world defined","summary":"Proclus reviews readings of 'according to one and according to genera' by Atticus, Amelius, Theodorus and Xenarchus, and prefers Iamblichus, who puts unity before multitude. Animal itself holds all things as a whole before its parts. Combining Plotinus and Theodorus, he places it at the end of the intelligibles and identifies it with the Orphic Phanes, born from the egg of ether and chaos. Answering Atticus and Porphyry, he says animal itself is prior to, in, and after the Demiurgus, who is contained in it as an effect in its cause. Intelligibles are united yet unconfused. The world receives likenesses only of things according to nature, and Plato's phrase defines it as one visible animal containing all animals.","ideas":["Animal itself is a whole prior to parts","Phanes from the Orphic egg corresponds to Plato's animal itself","The intelligible holds all things as a tetrad, the Demiurgus as a decad","There are no ideas of preternatural things"],"terms":[{"t":"Phanes","d":"Orphic first-born god from the cosmic egg, here equated with animal itself."},{"t":"pantotēs (allness)","d":"The quality of being all things, held differently by intelligible and intellectual orders."}]},{"i":926,"j":943,"loc":"Tim. 31a (lemmas: 'one world or many and infinite'; 'one, if fabricated according to the paradigm')","title":"Why there is only one world","summary":"Timaeus asks whether there is one world or many, and Proclus explains that oneness crowns the account by making the world a god. He sides with Porphyry and Iamblichus that the question has three options: one, finitely many, infinitely many. Plato gives the answer first: one, because made after one paradigm. Against the objection that one Form of man yields many men, Proclus weighs Porphyry's solution, which Iamblichus rejects, and Iamblichus's own, which he refines with bound and infinity. He gives Syrianus's answer that lower things imitate forms' essence and eternity but not their oneness, and adds his own. These are: the two fabrications; three senses of 'only-begotten'; and each form making a monad before its multitude.","ideas":["Oneness is the divine summit of the world's attributes","A single paradigm yields a single, only-begotten world","Mortal multiplicity comes from the junior gods' second fabrication","Forms proceed from monad to number to infinite multitude"],"terms":[{"t":"monogenēs (only-begotten)","d":"Unique of its kind; in the strict sense, having nothing coordinate with it."}]},{"i":944,"j":963,"loc":"Book II (on Timaeus 31a)","title":"One paradigm, one Demiurgus, one world","summary":"Proclus finishes explaining how the whole fabrication produces only monadic, perpetual natures, distinguishing three fabrications (Jovian, Dionysiacal, Adonaical) and leaving the partial multitude to later causes. Because both the paradigm and the Demiurgus are monads, the world is monadic, and Plato rests the proof chiefly on the paradigm as the more united cause. On the lemma that what comprehends all intelligible animals cannot be second with another, Proclus argues by cases that two co-arranged all-perfect animals would need a prior cause, so multitude must stop at a monad. He confirms this from the Orphic Phanes, who proceeds alone, and from the Oracles' fountain of fountains.","ideas":["Three monads: animal itself (intelligible), the Demiurgus (intellectual), the universe (sensible)","Two all-perfect animals would require a prior uniting cause, so the first is only-begotten","Unlike Heaven and Earth or Jupiter and Juno, Phanes proceeds alone"],"terms":[{"t":"monad","d":"A unitary cause standing prior to a multitude and containing it undivided."},{"t":"Phanes","d":"The Orphic god born from the egg, read by Proclus as animal itself, the intelligible paradigm."}]},{"i":964,"j":977,"loc":"Book II (on Timaeus 31a–b)","title":"Against infinite or many worlds","summary":"Proclus argues from the goodness of the Demiurgus that the world is the single image of all-perfect animal, partial paradigms having partial fabrications within it. On Plato's denial of two or infinite worlds he separates infinity of power, of multitude and of magnitude, and argues that infinite worlds would have no order or single cause. He interprets the barbaric scheme of 183 worlds reported by Plutarch symbolically, and adds Aristotle-style objections about up and down between worlds. Plato's own proof rests on the onlyness of the paradigm and, as Theophrastus notes, on providence rather than matter; Porphyry extends it to show there is one first principle. The book ends with 'one and only-begotten' read through the one, the one being and eternity.","ideas":["Infinite multitude has no first term and so no order or single producing cause","Plutarch's triangle of worlds may symbolize intellectual, psychical and physical series","Plato proves oneness from the paradigm, not from matter or place","'Was, is and will be' express temporal perpetuity imaging eternity"],"terms":[{"t":"only-begotten (monogenēs)","d":"Single in kind and comprehensive of all secondary natures, as the world is said to be."}]},{"i":978,"j":991,"loc":"Book III (on Timaeus 31b) (1 of 2)","title":"Second fabrication and the visible, tangible world","summary":"Book III opens by dividing fabrication into three: by wholeness, by division into wholes, and by division into parts, the last left to the junior Gods. Body is generated, intellect unbegotten, and soul a middle essence, and Plato now turns to the middle fabrication. Proclus lists the ten gifts the Demiurgus confers on the world, beginning with sensibility. Taking the lemma that the generated must be visible and tangible, he explains why the whole world counts as corporeal-formed and why fire and earth stand as extreme contraries. Against Theophrastus he holds that Plato names sight and touch because the world is seen and touched, and that the world sees and touches itself.","ideas":["Three fabrications: whole, into wholes, into parts","A decad of demiurgic gifts structures the account of the body of the world","Visible and tangible are the extremes of sensibles, as fire and earth are of elements"],"terms":[{"t":"corporeal-formed","d":"Said of a whole that has body in it, though not all of its parts are body."}]},{"i":992,"j":1004,"loc":"Book III (on Timaeus 31b) (2 of 2)","title":"Why fire makes visible and earth solid","summary":"Treating Plato's statement as an axiom, Proclus argues that nothing is visible without fire, since colours, sight and their meeting all depend on light. He separates many kinds of fire, from celestial light to subterranean streams, answering Aristotle's objection that the stars are visible without being fire. Earth is the first solid and the cause of solidity to the other elements, its peculiarity being tangibility rather than weight; Porphyry's report of touchable dæmons is cited. Against Aristotle on circular motion he gives Plotinus's view that a simple body in its own place either rests or moves in a circle, so celestial fire revolves.","ideas":["Fire is characterized by visibility, not by heat or upward motion","There are many grades of fire and of earth","Straight-line motion belongs to elements out of their natural place"],"terms":[{"t":"physical solid","d":"A resisting, tangible body, as distinct from the intangible mathematical solid."}]},{"i":1005,"j":1016,"loc":"Book III (on Timaeus 31b–c)","title":"Two things need a third as bond","summary":"On the lemma that two things cannot cohere beautifully without a third, Proclus dismisses objections drawn from semicircles, the monad and duad, wine mixed with honey, the union of man and woman, and melted metals. He distinguishes three kinds of bond: one in the cause, one organic in the things bound, and one in the middle. Plato means the middle kind, which is analogy, inherent in the things bound yet distinct from them. This bond derives from the one, the one being, all-perfect animal and the connecting cause. Fire and earth, as form and matter, are joined first by a medium such as moisture, so the world proceeds from duad to triad.","ideas":["A true bond preserves what it binds rather than destroying it","Threefold bond: causal, intermediate, organic","Contraries cannot unite themselves and need a third"],"terms":[{"t":"desmos (bond)","d":"What unites separated things into one while keeping them what they are."}]},{"i":1017,"j":1031,"loc":"Book III (on Timaeus 31c–32a)","title":"Analogy: the mathematical and physical bond","summary":"Proclus calls analogy the bond and shows how the arithmetic, geometric and harmonic means all arise from equality. He argues that Plato's lemma of three numbers, masses or powers defines the geometric mean, the only proper analogy, and assigns each mean to its usual domain, including musical sounds. He then insists that physics be joined to mathematics without being reduced to it. Physically, analogy is one life running through the world, binding numbers (material forms), masses and powers. The Orphic golden chain and Vulcan's binding of Venus and Mars are given as theological images of this bond.","ideas":["All three means are generated from equality","Geometric proportion alone is analogy in the strict sense","Physical analogy is a single life and reason running through the cosmos","Analogy leads through sameness to union"],"terms":[{"t":"analogia","d":"Sameness of ratio among terms, chiefly the geometric mean."},{"t":"mesotēs (middle, mean)","d":"An intermediate term binding extremes by a fixed relation."}]},{"i":1032,"j":1043,"loc":"Book III (on Timaeus 32a–b) (1 of 2)","title":"Planes need one mean, solids two","summary":"On the lemma that a plane would need one medium but solids two, Proclus first argues that the elements must be four: one element would allow no change, and two contraries need a medium, which for solids must be double. He then gives the mathematics: similar planes such as 9 and 16 have one mean, while cubes such as 8 and 27 have two (12 and 18). He answers objections, such as 64 and 729 having one mean as squares, and Democritus's doubt about planes. He holds that Plato means similar solids in rational ratios, and promises Archytas's construction of two mean proportionals.","ideas":["Generation needs contraries, contraries need a bond, solids need two bonds","Each mean takes two sides from the nearer extreme and one from the farther","Numbers both square and cube have one mean only as squares"],"terms":[]},{"i":1044,"j":1053,"loc":"Book III (on Timaeus 32a–b) (2 of 2)","title":"Why each element has three powers","summary":"Proclus turns to the physical sense, noting that Iamblichus treats simple forms as 'planes' and composite essences as 'solids'. He rejects physiologists who give each element one power, and Ocellus, who gives two (hot, cold, moist, dry), since that scheme makes fire and earth no more contrary than other pairs. Following Timæus, he gives each element three powers: fire is fine-parted, sharp and mobile, and earth is the opposite in all three. Air and water arise by exchanging one power each, so the series fire, air, water, earth forms a continuous proportion like 8, 12, 18, 27.","ideas":["Fire and earth are contrary in every power, as in place and motion","Air takes two powers from fire and one from earth; water the reverse","Plato's physics follows the mathematics of solid numbers"],"terms":[{"t":"three powers","d":"Fineness or grossness of parts, sharpness or bluntness, ease or difficulty of motion."}]},{"i":1054,"j":1063,"loc":"Book III (on Timaeus 32b) (1 of 2)","title":"Are the heavens made of the four?","summary":"On the lemma placing water and air between fire and earth, Proclus weighs Platonists who give the heavens a fifth body after Aristotle against those who limit Plato to the sublunary elements. He sets out the dilemmas and concludes that heaven is mainly fire but contains the other elements causally, in their summits: celestial earth shows itself in the moon's shadow. He then traces how the elements come forth from the Demiurgus, whose body in an Orphic verse is fire, air, sea and earth. They pass by steps from intellectual powers to self-moving lives, to living beings, to ordered bodies moved by another, and finally to disordered dregs.","ideas":["Heaven is fire-dominated but holds all four elements","Celestial fire does not burn and celestial earth is not heavy","The elements descend through graded media from the Demiurgus"],"terms":[{"t":"fifth body","d":"Aristotle's ether, a distinct substance for the heavens."}]},{"i":1064,"j":1073,"loc":"Book III (on Timaeus 32b) (2 of 2)","title":"Elements in the heavens; inexact analogy","summary":"Proclus argues that elements exist in as many ways as there are media between the Demiurgus and the sublunary world, and that causes contain beforehand the powers of what they govern. He reports a Pythagorean assignment of elements to the planets but rejects it as un-Platonic in its order. Three mixtures yield the heavens (fiery), the sublunary region, and the subterranean rivers, so heaven is in a sense a fifth essence mingled from the four. Plato's 'from all fire' and the Chaldean Oracles agree. Physical analogy is less exact than mathematical analogy, which is why Plato says 'as much as possible'.","ideas":["Whatever governs a thing must contain its power in advance","Heaven, earth and underworld are three mixtures of the four elements","Geometric analogy alone gives unbroken order"],"terms":[]},{"i":1074,"j":1083,"loc":"Book III (on Timaeus 32b–c)","title":"Friendship of the elements; nothing left outside","summary":"Proclus reads the four elements as the intelligible tetrad unfolding from monad through duad, with the Pythagorean tetractys and decad as witnesses. The world's friendship with itself, which preserves it, comes from analogy, nature, soul, intellect, the Gods and the Demiurgus, who throws round it the Oracles' 'bond of love'. He lists six ways a thing can be destroyed (from its maker, form or matter, each in two ways) and shows the world escapes all of them; only its binder could undo it, and he only binds. In the next lemma the world contains every whole and power of each element, distributed to heaven, the middle region and the underworld under Jupiter, Neptune and Pluto.","ideas":["The world's self-friendship is its self-preservation","None of the six modes of destruction applies to the world","A part of an element is the same in kind; a power is one of its properties"],"terms":[{"t":"philia (friendship)","d":"The concord among the world's parts that keeps the whole in being."}]},{"i":1084,"j":1099,"loc":"Book III (on Timaeus 32c–33a)","title":"Perfect, one, and free from age","summary":"On the lemma that the Demiurgus reasoned the world would be perfect, one, and free from age and disease, Proclus links perfection, unity and perpetuity, each derived from the paradigm. He explains why soul-vehicles are troubled by matter, what Plato's 'dianoia' in the Demiurgus means, and how whole and part are ranked. The world is free from disease because the Demiurgus holds the fountain of health, both creative and Æsculapian renewing health. Heat and cold stand for any strong powers that could attack from outside. One matter descending by degrees explains why there is one world, and things perish only when similar bodies exist outside them.","ideas":["Nothing outside the world means there is nothing to corrupt it","Two kinds of health: creative and renewing","Multiplying worlds would be weakness, not power"],"terms":[{"t":"dianoia","d":"Discursive reasoning, here Plato's unfolding of the Demiurgus's single intellection."}]},{"i":1100,"j":1114,"loc":"Book III (on Timaeus 33a–b)","title":"The spherical figure: Platonic demonstrations","summary":"Proclus briefly glosses the world as one whole of wholes, ageless and free from disease. He then takes up the gift of figure, the fourth demiurgic gift: the sphere, which contains all figures and is most like itself. He offers three proofs, from the one, from beauty or fitness, and from kinship, citing Parmenides' sphere and Empedocles' two spheres, and finds the spherical in intelligible, intellectual and demiurgic orders, down to Vulcan. Further arguments follow: the sphere is most capacious and can hold the five regular solids, it masters by likeness, and, following Iamblichus, it images the circles of the world soul.","ideas":["Among figures the sphere is what the one is among Gods","The sphere can contain all regular solids","Circular body imitates intellect; straight motion imitates souls' descent"],"terms":[]},{"i":1115,"j":1128,"loc":"Book III (on Timaeus 33b)","title":"Sphericity proved; the world's smooth surface","summary":"Proclus adds an Aristotelian physical proof (any other figure would leave a void as it revolved) and others from the nesting of the elements, from simplicity and from speed. Astronomers' proofs follow, from parallel star circles, unequal days, shadows and the stars' constant size. Returning to Plato's words, he places the sphere intelligibly, intellectually and sensibly, and defends the claim that the similar is better than the dissimilar against Platonists who put difference above sameness. On the lemma of the smooth outer surface, smoothness signals that the world needs no organs, is luminous like Olympus, and receives soul and intellect as a mirror receives images, as in Vulcan's mirror for Bacchus.","ideas":["Similitude belongs to forms, dissimilitude comes in from matter","Smoothness signifies fitness to receive divine illumination","The sensible world is called 'spheriform', imitating a higher sphere"],"terms":[{"t":"sphairoeidēs (spheriform)","d":"Sphere-like, Plato's term for the sensible world as an image of the true sphere."}]},{"i":1129,"j":1140,"loc":"Book III (on Timaeus 33c)","title":"Does the world have senses?","summary":"On the lemma that the world needs no eyes or ears, Proclus says Plato removes the partial organs of a life lived in generation. Weighing theologians who give sense to the gods against the Theaetetus, which calls sense passive, he takes a middle way. He sets out four grades of sense: one like intellect that holds its object within, a second that is outward but impassive, a third mixed with passion, and a fourth, that of plants. The world has the first kind: it is at once the seen, the eye and the seeing. Like the 'eyeless Love' of Orpheus, it sees its own beauty and hears its own harmony without organs.","ideas":["Four grades of sense from consciousness-like to plant-like","The world is sensible to itself as a whole","Our common sense derives from the world's one sense"],"terms":[{"t":"co-sensation","d":"The whole's awareness of itself, closer to consciousness than to sense."}]},{"i":1141,"j":1149,"loc":"Book III (on Timaeus 33c–34a)","title":"No breath, food, hands or feet","summary":"Plato now removes smell (through respiration) and taste (through nutrition), and Proclus explains why he ties each sense to the more inclusive function. The world feeds on its own consumption: elements pass into each other, and heavenly bodies wax and wane only in analogy, with the moon as the isthmus between them. Proclus rejects the view that the stars feed on exhalations. Self-sufficiency joins the world more closely to its maker. Hands and feet are unnecessary since nothing lies outside to grasp, repel or move towards, but the world has divine powers that answer to such organs.","ideas":["The world nourishes itself from its own parts","Celestial bodies are not fed by exhalations","Corruption within the world is not simply evil, since the world harms nothing of its own","Its guardian, perfective and vivific powers are its 'hands', 'nutrition' and 'breath'"],"terms":[{"t":"autarkeia (self-sufficiency)","d":"Needing nothing external, an element of the good."}]},{"i":1150,"j":1161,"loc":"Book III (on Timaeus 34a)","title":"Circular motion: the sixth gift","summary":"Against views that the world is moved only by love of the first good, or is turned only from outside, Proclus holds that Plato gives it a motion of its own, granted by the Demiurgus. Of the seven local motions, circular motion has no contrary and belongs most to intellect and wisdom; the other six are linked to soul and the hexad. On 'according to sameness, in the same, in itself', he finds perpetuity, immutability and the passage of parts into each other's places. He criticizes Severus for accepting the myth of reversed revolutions. The world is truly unwandering, and the Demiurgus of body is called lame in both feet because the world has no legs.","ideas":["Circulation imitates intellect; the world's essence imitates the intelligible","Degrees of wandering run from underground streams to intellect","The universe moves in place by exchange of its parts"],"terms":[{"t":"inerratic (aplanēs)","d":"Non-wandering, keeping one uniform motion."}]},{"i":1162,"j":1168,"loc":"Book III (on Timaeus 34a–b)","title":"Recapitulation: the God who would once exist","summary":"Proclus reads the summary lemma as gathering the bodily fabrication into one and maps each gift (number and bond, wholeness, sphericity, self-motion, soul, intellect) to a higher divine cause. 'Eternally existing God' shows the Demiurgus is eternal and so not a soul, since soul is indestructible but not eternal. 'The God that would once exist' does not mean a beginning in time, as Atticus thought: the world exists in time, whose whole is 'once' compared with eternity. Alternatively, 'once' refers to the world as built up in discourse. Smooth, equal from the middle and perfect from perfect bodies recall the earlier gifts.","ideas":["The Demiurgus belongs among eternal beings","'Once' marks temporal as against eternal existence","Discourse divides what the cause produces at once"],"terms":[]},{"i":1169,"j":1181,"loc":"Book III (on Timaeus 34b) (1 of 2)","title":"Soul placed in the middle","summary":"Proclus explains that the Demiurgus produces everything at once, but the account can run downward by procession or upward by conversion; Plato now proceeds by conversion, so body is treated before soul. Animation is the seventh gift. He reviews the 'middle' as centre of the earth, moon, sun, fixed sphere, equator or zodiac, and Porphyry's and Iamblichus's non-spatial readings. He favours Syrianus's reading: the world soul animates the centre and each sphere through different powers and keeps an exempt power outside the universe. The works of placing, extending and veiling express the Demiurgus's divine cause of animation.","ideas":["Procession and conversion order the account differently","Soul's guardian power holds the centre, the 'tower of Jupiter'","Plato says 'soul' in the middle, not 'the soul'"],"terms":[{"t":"conversion (epistrophē)","d":"Return from effects to their cause, here the upward order of exposition."}]},{"i":1182,"j":1192,"loc":"Book III (on Timaeus 34b) (2 of 2)","title":"One, single, solitary: a blessed God","summary":"'Circle revolving in a circle' is read by Porphyry as a circular body turning about its centre, and better by Iamblichus as the bodily circle moved within the soul's circle. The three names one, single and solitary come from the final, paradigmatic and demiurgic causes, and show the world to be a God. Solitude means self-sufficiency through virtue: like a good man, the world can live with itself and love itself. Proclus adds that intellect must therefore know itself. He ranks forms of happiness from the world, through the mundane Gods, the higher kinds and undefiled souls, down to partial souls and animals.","ideas":["Virtue, self-knowledge and self-friendship come from soul, intellect and deity","The one, perfect and self-sufficient make something a God","Each thing is deified through what comes just before it"],"terms":[{"t":"eudaimōn (happy)","d":"Living under one's proper dæmon, here the Demiurgus as great dæmon."}]},{"i":1193,"j":1207,"loc":"Book III (on Timaeus 34b–35a)","title":"Soul older than body, and its ruler","summary":"Plato corrects the impression that soul was made after body: we speak from the lower to the higher because, fallen into generation, we see effects first. Proclus takes older and younger in order of essence, not time, against Atticus, and notes that 'the soul' here means the mundane soul. Our habit of reasoning upward reflects the 'casual' element we take on through sense. On soul as prior in generation and virtue, he treats generation as essential procession and separates 'prior' (order) from 'more ancient' (causal superiority). As despot and ruler, soul is by essence lord of body, which shows the world has always been ensouled.","ideas":["Fallen souls judge from secondary things; abiding souls from first ones","Soul is unbegotten in time but proceeds from intelligible causes","Ruling is essential to soul, so world and soul coexist always"],"terms":[{"t":"despot and ruler","d":"The lord serves its own good; the ruler serves the good of those it rules."}]},{"i":1208,"j":1226,"loc":"Book III (on Timaeus 35a) (1 of 2)","title":"Why an unbegotten soul has a generation","summary":"On the lemma of the soul's mixture from impartible and partible essence, Proclus lists seven questions and starts with Theophrastus's objection to seeking the cause of a first principle. He answers that the Peripatetics stop at moving souls or intellects, whereas Plato puts intellects, Gods and the one before soul. Soul is not first: it is multiple, not present in all things, and capable of evil. It is generated because it lives through time and receives its power endlessly, while being in another sense eternal. He divides the inquiry into five heads (hyparxis, harmony, form, powers, energies) and matches them to the five-part account of the world's body.","ideas":["Seeking the 'why' marks scientific knowledge","Soul is both unbegotten and always coming to be","Soul is harmony of itself, not of something else as in the Phaedo"],"terms":[{"t":"hyparxis","d":"The bare existence or first ground of a thing's essence."}]},{"i":1227,"j":1233,"loc":"Book III (on Timaeus 35a) (2 of 2)","title":"Soul's middle rank and the five genera","summary":"Proclus lists the marks of intelligibles (true being, eternity, immobility) and of sensibles (becoming, partibility, being moved by another), and sets soul between them as self-moving, eternal in essence and temporal in activity. Soul is the boundary of intelligibles and the beginning of sensibles, imitating the goddess who causes it. Its whole has infinite power and its parts are always coming to be, so it is both unbegotten and generated. He then begins the fourth question: forms range from atomic to most total, and the most total are the five genera of the Sophist (essence, sameness, difference, motion, permanency) of which all beings partake.","ideas":["Soul binds beings as a mean within them, not apart from them","As moved things are to self-movers, so self-movers are to unmoved","Essence is the Vesta of the genera; sameness and difference answer to bound and infinity"],"terms":[{"t":"five genera","d":"Essence, sameness, difference, motion, permanency, the widest kinds of being."}]},{"i":1234,"j":1248,"loc":"Tim. 35a: the genera of being in the soul","title":"The five genera and how souls share them","summary":"Proclus finishes his account of the five genera of being (essence, sameness, difference, motion, permanency) and shows that every being has essence, power and energy through them, though they predominate differently in intelligibles, intellects, souls and bodies. Turning to the fifth head, he argues that the soul must be made from the genera of being because it knows all beings, intelligible and sensible. Plato builds the soul from the middle genera, between the impartible genera of intelligibles and the partible ones of bodies. Each genus has impartible, partible and middle forms depending on whether bound or infinity prevails, and the soul, like number, divides into finite rather than infinite parts.","ideas":["Every being shares essence, sameness, difference, motion and permanency, in different modes at each level","The soul is made from the genera because it must know all things","Bound or infinity prevailing yields impartible, partible or middle genera","Mixtures where essence, sameness or difference predominates yield divine, angelic, daemoniacal, heroic and human souls"],"terms":[{"t":"genera of being","d":"Essence, sameness, difference, motion and permanency, the most universal causes that all beings share as beings."},{"t":"hyparxis","d":"The first, essential existence of a thing, which Proclus places before its power and energy."}]},{"i":1249,"j":1266,"loc":"Tim. 35a: the impartible and the partible essence","title":"What the impartible and partible essences are","summary":"Proclus first defines the two essences generally: everything intellectual and separate is impartible, and everything that proceeds about bodies (nature, sense, physical reasons) is partible, with soul in between. Speaking more exactly, he says the world soul is the middle between the one mundane intellect and the life divisible about the body of the universe, and that each soul, solar, lunar or daemonic, is a middle between its own proper extremes. He sets out the views of Theodorus and Iamblichus, who refer the passage to the supermundane soul, and asks what partible and impartible mean for supermundane and human souls. He then matches the doctrine to the Orphic laceration of Bacchus, in which the undivided heart is intellect, and closes by answering a doubt from the second hypothesis of the Parmenides.","ideas":["The world soul is a middle between its own participated intellect and the corporeal-formed life of the universe","Every soul has its own proximate extremes, not all extremes everywhere","Intellect is like the sun, soul like its light, partible life like the splendour from that light","In the Orphic myth the heart of Bacchus left undivided by Minerva stands for the impartible intellect"],"terms":[{"t":"impartible essence","d":"For Proclus here, the intellect participated by the world soul, which is one and undivided."},{"t":"essence partible about bodies","d":"The corporeal-formed life that proceeds from soul and is divided along with the bodies it lives in."}]},{"i":1267,"j":1278,"loc":"Tim. 35a (\"From an essence always impartible…\")","title":"The middle form of essence and rival readings","summary":"On the lemma, Proclus argues that the soul is a middle not only between impartible and partible but between eternal being and that which comes to be, since it is eternal in essence but temporal in its intellections. He rules out that both extremes lie in the soul or both outside it, and shows that the soul is made not from the extremes themselves, which cannot mix, but from things analogous to them. The words \"middle\", \"mingled\" and \"form of essence\" are explained as signs of the soul's self-constitution and of genus and species in the Demiurgus. He rejects Eratosthenes and Severus, who gave the soul geometrical interval, then lists and refutes the readings of Numenius, Plutarch and Atticus, Plotinus and Theodorus.","ideas":["The soul is the first of generated natures yet eternal in its essence","The soul is made from analogues of the extremes, not from the extremes mixed together","The soul is one and not one: many essences kept within a preserved unity","Earlier views (monad and duad, point and interval, rational and irrational parts, intellect and sense) are each found wanting"],"terms":[{"t":"psychogony","d":"Plato's account of the generation of the soul in the Timaeus."},{"t":"form of essence","d":"The soul's essence as a species of the genus of essence that exists in the Demiurgus."}]},{"i":1279,"j":1286,"loc":"Tim. 35a–b (same, different, \"three beings\", \"by force\")","title":"Mixing sameness and difference in the soul","summary":"Proclus comments on three lemmas. Sameness ranks above difference, against Platonists who rate difference higher, and the soul has a middle sameness and a middle difference, not sameness from intelligibles and difference from sensibles. Each genus has impartible, partible and middle species, and Plato's \"after the same manner\" marks the middle as a form, not a composite whole. \"Taking them as three\" shows the three genera were held separately in the Demiurgus before he mixed them into one idea, making the soul a form of forms. Difference is hard to mix because it separates even itself, so the Demiurgus first fits it to sameness and then mixes both with essence, and his \"force\" means an abundance of power, not violence.","ideas":["Sameness is better than difference","Every genus exists in impartible, partible and middle forms","Mixing order: difference fitted to sameness, then both mixed with essence","Divine force is an excess of power, not a force against nature"],"terms":[{"t":"idea","d":"The single form that results when the Demiurgus unites the three middle genera."}]},{"i":1287,"j":1296,"loc":"Tim. 35b (\"having mingled these two with essence…\")","title":"One whole from three, then divided","summary":"Proclus says sameness and difference go with essence as equal and unequal go with quantity, and he praises Plato above the physicists, Aristotle included, for grounding contraries in sameness and difference rather than in excess and defect. He explains why Plato speaks of the soul as a middle in essences rather than in powers, forms or numbers, and outlines a fourfold scale of number and of harmony. Following Porphyry and his own teacher, he places the second mixture, but not the making of the elements, in the Crater. The division into parts comes from an intellectual cause: the whole stays whole, the soul is one and many like the continuity of time, and every part holds all three genera.","ideas":["Contraries are traced back to sameness and difference, which exist in the Demiurgus","The soul's harmony is a middle between harmony itself and harmony in harmonized things","The first mixture is outside the Crater, the second inside it","Division leaves the whole intact; each part contains essence, sameness and difference"],"terms":[{"t":"Crater","d":"The mixing bowl of the Timaeus, which Proclus links with the life-giving goddess (Juno) who works with the Demiurgus."}]},{"i":1297,"j":1303,"loc":"Tim. 35b: harmonic preliminaries","title":"Harmonic basics and the three means","summary":"Before the text on the parts of the soul, Proclus sets out the harmonic facts it assumes: the Pythagorean concords as ratios (fourth 4:3, fifth 3:2, octave 2:1), the tone as 9:8, and the leimma. He names the three genera of melody and argues that Plato uses the diatonic, defending this against Aristoxenus by appeal to Adrastus. He explains why the diagram reaches four octaves, a fifth and a tone, and outlines the three stages of the passage: the seven parts, the insertion of two means, and the filling with tones and leimmas. He then defines the arithmetic, harmonic and geometric means, shows how to find them, and proves that in a four-term proportion an arithmetic mean pairs with a harmonic mean.","ideas":["Concords are taken from multiple and superparticular ratios","Plato's division is diatonic: tones and leimmas, not enharmonic dieses","The diagram runs to 27 so that the soul reaches solid numbers and can rule bodies","The arithmetic and harmonic means are contained in the geometric"],"terms":[{"t":"leimma","d":"The remainder of a fourth after two tones (256:243), slightly less than a true semitone."},{"t":"sesquioctave","d":"The ratio 9:8, the interval of a tone."},{"t":"diatonic genus","d":"The melodic genus that divides a fourth into semitone (leimma), tone and tone."}]},{"i":1304,"j":1318,"loc":"Tim. 35b–36b: building the diagram","title":"Constructing the numbers of the soul diagram","summary":"On the lemma about the first, double and triple parts, Proclus says the mathematics must be neither scorned nor taken alone, and he works it through. He sets out the seven terms 1, 2, 3, 4, 9, 8, 27 and multiplies them by 6 so that harmonic and arithmetic means can be inserted. Finding that 6 is not enough to divide the fourths into tones and leimmas, he moves to 384 as the first term and to 10368 as the last. He then shows that the leimma (256:243) is less than a semitone, works out the apotome, and proves that 256:243 is the remainder of a fourth after two tones, in least terms.","ideas":["The seven terms are arranged in one line, duple and triple ratios alternating","The base number grows from 6 to 384 so every interval can be filled in whole numbers","A tone cannot be split into two equal halves; leimma and apotome are unequal","The leimma 256:243 is proved to be what remains of 4:3 after two tones"],"terms":[{"t":"apotome","d":"The larger part of a tone left after the leimma, in the ratio 2187:2048."}]},{"i":1319,"j":1327,"loc":"Tim. 35b–36b: comma, terms and diagrams","title":"The comma and the full set of terms","summary":"Proclus finds the comma (531441:524288), the gap between apotome and leimma, and notes the Pythagorean and Ptolemaic disagreements with Aristoxenus. He then fills every double and triple interval from 384 to 10368 with means, tones and leimmas, giving 34 terms with 24 tones and 9 leimmas across four octaves, a fifth and a tone. He reconciles this with the 36 terms of the Pythagorean Timæus, explained by inserting apotomes that Plato does not use. He proves that the arithmetic mean of a double interval is the harmonic mean of the triple, reports Severus' diagram ending in a leimma, and prefers a straight-line layout to Adrastus' λ-shaped one, since no number in the soul should appear twice.","ideas":["The complete diagram has 34 terms, 24 tones and 9 leimmas","The 36 terms of the Pythagorean Timæus add the apotome, which Plato's diatonic does not use","Severus ends the diagram in a leimma rather than a tone","A straight line is better than a λ because no part of the soul is repeated"],"terms":[{"t":"comma","d":"The small interval by which the apotome exceeds the leimma."}]},{"i":1328,"j":1340,"loc":"Tim. 35b–36b: meaning of the division","title":"What the division of the soul means","summary":"Turning from mathematics to meaning, Proclus denies that the division is bodily, numerical, like seed-reasons, or like theorems of a science. It is an immaterial, demiurgic division in which the whole remains. He distinguishes three wholenesses of the soul (before the parts, from the parts, and in each part) and shows that Plato keeps a unit-and-pair structure throughout. Dividing is Dionysian and harmonizing is Apollonian, which links the seven parts to the seven pieces of Bacchus torn by the Titans. The three means are images of Themis' daughters Eunomia, Justice and Peace, and they bind the soul's essence, samenesses and differences respectively.","ideas":["The soul's division is intellectual and leaves its wholeness intact","Three wholenesses: before the parts, from the parts, in each part","Dividing is Dionysian, harmonizing Apollonian; seven belongs to both gods","Geometric mean binds essences, harmonic binds samenesses, arithmetic binds differences"],"terms":[{"t":"hebdomad","d":"The number seven, which Proclus ties to Apollo and to the sevenfold division of Bacchus."},{"t":"Eunomia, Dice, Peace","d":"The daughters of Themis, of whom the geometric, harmonic and arithmetic means are images."}]},{"i":1341,"j":1357,"loc":"Tim. 35b–36b: ratios, parts and cosmic harmony","title":"The seven ratios and seven parts","summary":"Proclus says the soul holds seven simple kinds of ratio (equality, multiple, superparticular, superpartient and their inverses) and links them to the ways forms are shared, down to mixed creatures like the mule. He interprets the seven parts: the first is the soul's one summit, the double its going-forth, the triple its return, the fourth and fifth (planes) the causes of incorporeal things divided about bodies, and the sixth and seventh (cubes) the causes of bodies. The means and the sesquialter, sesquitertian and sesquioctave ratios bind the soul ever more partially. The diagram's four octaves, fifth and tone answer to the world's division into four, five, eight and nine, with the Muses and Syrens presiding, and the leimma gathers the last dregs of the elements below the earth.","ideas":["Seven simple ratios correspond to the seven parts; the soul is sevenfold throughout","First part abides, second proceeds, third returns; squares and cubes cause what follows soul","Harmony is in intellect as monad, in soul as tetrad, in the world as decad","The leimma is the cause of the lowest, subterranean part of the cosmic harmony"],"terms":[{"t":"superparticular ratio","d":"A ratio in which the larger term exceeds the smaller by one part of it, such as 3:2, 4:3 or 9:8."},{"t":"superpartient ratio","d":"A ratio in which the larger term exceeds the smaller by several parts, such as the leimma 256:243."}]},{"i":1358,"j":1367,"loc":"Tim. 35b (\"He first took one part from the whole…\")","title":"Earlier interpreters of the seven terms","summary":"Going back to the lemma, Proclus insists it be read philosophically, since the numbers only picture the soul's essence. He rejects readings that map the seven terms onto the planetary spheres, their distances, speeds or sizes: Plato never gives such figures, the subject is the soul's generation and not the world's, and the order of the terms (9 before 8) does not fit. He reports Amelius, who links the numbers to gods, daemons, humans and animals; Porphyry, who argues the soul must be diatonically harmonized; and Iamblichus, who reads the numbers as permanence, procession and return. Theodorus of Asine is reported at length, with his three souls and his numbers for the elements and the heavens.","ideas":["Astronomical readings of the terms conflict with the text and with astronomy","Amelius: unit for gods, two and three for daemons, four and nine for humans, eight and twenty-seven for the rest","Porphyry: a multitude made by intellect must be numbered and diatonically harmonized","Theodorus assigns 7 to earth, 9 to water, 13 to air, 11 to fire"],"terms":[]},{"i":1368,"j":1378,"loc":"Tim. 35b–c (the seven parts, per Syrianus)","title":"Syrianus' reading of the seven parts","summary":"Proclus finds Theodorus elegant but unable to reach the means and leimmas, and gives his teacher's reading instead. The soul as a whole abides by the first part, proceeds by the double, returns by the triple, and cares for what comes after it: the fourth and fifth parts govern things that share in it next, the eighth and twenty-seventh the solid bodies. As a multitude it has ratios that are total, generic, specific and partial, and the means joined to the geometric secure justice. Proclus then reads the text word by word: the parts were taken away from a soul that already held them; the unit is the root of the whole; the double is procession and the triple return; and in the lemma on the fourth to seventh parts, even and odd cubes stand for procession and return in bodies.","ideas":["Three processions and three returns around one abiding summit","Even numbers mark procession, odd numbers mark return","The geometric mean must govern the other two means, or justice fails","The unit is the ruling root of the soul, not the smallest quantity"],"terms":[{"t":"conversion (epistrophē)","d":"The return of what has proceeded back to its cause, here pictured by the triple and odd terms."}]},{"i":1379,"j":1388,"loc":"Tim. 36a (means and sesquioctave intervals)","title":"Filling the intervals with means and tones","summary":"On the lemma about filling the double and triple intervals, Proclus reads the cutting and binding as demiurgic \"sections and bonds\", and the word \"still\" as showing ranks of reasons within the soul. On the two means, he says Plato puts the harmonic mean before the arithmetic as nearer the geometric: the harmonic binds the soul's samenesses and the heavens, and the arithmetic binds its differences and the sublunary elements. On the sesquialter, sesquitertian and sesquioctave intervals, he says these ratios order parts of parts in the cosmos. He relates the ratios of each sphere, its divine, daemonic, heroic and human animals, to the fourth and fifth, with the leimma between human and daemonic souls.","ideas":["The Demiurgus' dividing and binding powers are theological sections and bonds","Harmonic mean rules celestial sameness; arithmetic mean rules sublunary exchange","Multiple, superparticular and superpartient ratios match three ways of sharing","Each sphere holds a fourth (and a fifth) among its kinds of living beings"],"terms":[{"t":"harmonic mean","d":"A middle term exceeding one extreme and exceeded by the other by the same fraction of each extreme, as 4 between 3 and 6."}]},{"i":1389,"j":1399,"loc":"Tim. 36b (\"leaving a part of each… 256 to 243\")","title":"The leimma and the number of the soul","summary":"Proclus explains the leimma in the cosmos as the cause of the material dregs and outflows of the elements that collect beneath the earth, which the world soul must still order, as the Orphic lines about Tartarus suggest. He reads the digits of 256 and 243 and the number thirteen symbolically. He then explains the 34 or 36 terms: 36 is the square of the hexad of soul, and 34 joins monad, tetrad and decad or sums 16 and 18. Four octaves, a fifth and a tone match the world's divisions by four ideas, five figures, eight spheres and nine parts, and the Demiurgus used up the mixture exactly. He gives 105947 as the number of the soul's essential monads and says how this number exists in divine, daemonic and human souls.","ideas":["The leimma is the soul's cause of the last, most material part of the world","34 and 36 terms are each shown to suit the soul","The diagram's concords match divisions by ideas, figures and spheres","The mixture was neither too much nor too little for the whole soul"],"terms":[{"t":"leimma (as cosmic cause)","d":"For Proclus, the ratio by which the soul orders the subterranean remnants of the elements."}]},{"i":1400,"j":1421,"loc":"Tim. 36b–c (the lengthwise split and the χ)","title":"The two strips and the X","summary":"Proclus first shows the figure mathematically: a strip holding all the numbers is cut lengthwise so that each half has every ratio, and the halves are crossed obliquely like the equator and zodiac. He then argues the soul is at once continuous and discrete, arithmetical and geometrical, and holds all the mathematical sciences without figure. He reports Iamblichus' reading of the two strips as two souls coming from a supermundane soul, but holds that Plato is speaking of the world soul alone, which has two lives, dianoetic and doxastic. The straight line pictures the soul's procession and transitive life, with intellect as point and centre, and he answers Peripatetic objections. Porphyry is cited for an Egyptian symbol, the X within a circle, and Plato's \"as it were\" marks the figure as a veil.","ideas":["Cutting \"according to length\" keeps every ratio in both strips","The soul is both continuous and discrete; intellect is point and centre to it","Plato means the world soul with two lives, not Iamblichus' supermundane souls","Mathematical names are veils for the truth, like the fables of theologians"],"terms":[{"t":"dianoetic and doxastic","d":"Discursive reason and opinion, the two lives of the soul pictured by the two strips."},{"t":"chi (χ)","d":"The X formed by the crossed strips, which Proclus calls a symbol of the soul."}]},{"i":1422,"j":1440,"loc":"Tim. 36c (circles, motion of the same, outer and inner)","title":"Bending into circles; circles of same and different","summary":"On bending the strips into circles, Proclus says this stands for the soul's return to its source, and that line and circle exist together in it, with no width or magnitude. He rejects taking the circles as rings. On the motion turning \"in the same\", he follows Iamblichus in making it the motion of the participated mundane intellect around the soul, not that of the Demiurgus. On the outer and inner circles, he disagrees with Iamblichus and makes them the soul's dianoetic and doxastic lives, the outer more divine. He answers Porphyry's objection about uniform parts with the image of immaterial mixture, like many lamps making one light. The Demiurgus' naming of the circles is creative and marks the soul's character, as in the Orphic question to Night.","ideas":["Straight line and circle remain together, as procession and return","The motion of the same belongs to the participated mundane intellect","The outer circle is dianoetic, the inner doxastic","Immaterial mixture keeps the mixed genera distinct, so one can predominate in one place"],"terms":[{"t":"circle of the same","d":"The outer, undivided circle of the soul, linked with sameness, intellect and the fixed stars."},{"t":"circle of the different","d":"The inner circle, linked with difference, opinion and the planets."}]},{"i":1441,"j":1451,"loc":"Tim. 36c (right and left, side and diagonal, dominion)","title":"Right and left, side and diagonal","summary":"Proclus treats the next lemma as about the soul's powers. Right, left, side and diagonal belong to the better and worse columns of opposites. Against reading right and left as merely relative to us, he holds, with partial agreement from Aristotle, that they are real properties put in by the Demiurgus: the fixed sphere turns to the right as the source of motion, the planets to the left. In the world soul the right faces intelligibles and the left cares for sensibles. He separates moving \"towards\" right or left (circular) from moving \"on\" right or left (straight-line). The side stands for the rational and comprehending, the diagonal for the irrational and dividing, and on the dominion lemma he concludes that sameness rules difference.","ideas":["Right and left in the cosmos are real properties, not just positions relative to us","Fixed sphere: right, side, sameness; planetary sphere: left, diagonal, difference","The diagonal is irrational and oblique, an image of the infinite","The circle of the same is undivided and rules; sameness is better than difference"],"terms":[{"t":"inerratic sphere","d":"The sphere of the fixed stars, moved by the circle of the same."}]},{"i":1452,"j":1462,"loc":"Tim. 36d (the seven unequal circles)","title":"The seven planetary circles in the soul","summary":"On the division of the inner circle into seven, Proclus surveys the astronomical explanations of contrary motion. He notes that Plato names no epicycles or eccentrics: the contrariety is between the one outer circle and the seven, with the Sun, Venus and Mercury moving at equal speed and the Moon, Saturn, Mars and Jupiter at unequal speeds. He explains how every circle keeps all the intervals and says the soul holds the causes of the spheres as nature holds the number of fingers. The three equal planets correspond to truth, beauty and symmetry, and the four to permanency, motion, sameness and difference. Monads and heptads are found in every element, and planets and elements are matched with pairs of gods. Seven fits the soul because of the life-giving goddess, and seven is also productive of due season.","ideas":["The contrary courses are those of the outer circle against the seven inner ones","The world soul contains the causes of the spheres before the planets exist","Sun, Venus, Mercury: truth, beauty, symmetry; the other four: four genera of being","Every element has its own monad and heptad, prefigured in the soul's circles"],"terms":[{"t":"hebdomad of circles","d":"The seven circles formed from the circle of the different, causes of the planetary spheres."}]},{"i":1463,"j":1471,"loc":"Tim. 34b–36d: the soul and the divine orders","title":"The soul's composition and the divine orders","summary":"Proclus relates each stage of the soul's making to an order of gods: its rule over body to the first principle, its three genera to the intelligibles, its self-begotten number to the summit of the intelligible-intellectual gods, its three means to the connective gods, its lines and circles to the triad of intellectual figure, its sevenfold powers to the intellectual hebdomad, and its two kinds of activity to the ruling and liberated gods. He rejects Platonists who divide the soul into one plus seven separate celestial or supercelestial souls. He reports Iamblichus' attack on Theodorus and Numenius for building psychogony from letters and their shapes. Finally he refers readers to his own treatise against Aristotle's objections, where he argues that the soul is not a magnitude.","ideas":["Each feature of the soul is an image of a divine order","The one world soul is divided by powers, not into separate souls","Iamblichus: counting letters and reading characters is arbitrary and proves nothing","The soul is not a circle in magnitude, as Aristotle's objection supposes"],"terms":[{"t":"liberated Gods","d":"The order of gods who both touch and do not touch the universe, to whom Proclus refers the soul's double activity."}]},{"i":1472,"j":1477,"loc":"Tim. 36d–e (the body within the soul)","title":"The world body framed within soul","summary":"Proclus says the passage reaches the last of five heads, the soul's activities, and shows it concerns the world soul, not supermundane souls. He explains each phrase: \"according to intellect\" points to the paradigm, \"in the next place\" means order, not time, and \"within the soul\" shows the world is the soul's offspring and vehicle, unlike the bodies partial souls receive. The corporeal nature is framed in the circles by the middle of the Demiurgus' three fabrications, the division into spheres, so the sublunary falls within the circle of the moon. On \"middle to middle\" he rejects Porphyry and says the body is placed in the middle of the soul, so it is animated evenly. On the last lemma, the soul is woven through from centre to edge and covers the world as with a veil.","ideas":["The world soul makes its own vehicle with the Demiurgus and governs it without labour","The world body is framed by dividing it into spheres that image the soul's circles","Body at the soul's middle means it is animated evenly from all sides","The soul fills the world from within and covers it from outside, like Minerva's aegis"],"terms":[{"t":"corporeal-formed nature","d":"The whole bodily nature of the universe, which the Demiurgus frames within the soul."}]},{"i":1478,"j":1489,"loc":"Book III, on Tim. 36e (1 of 2)","title":"Soul interwoven with the world and self-revolving","summary":"Proclus explains how the world-soul is interwoven with the universe and covers it outwardly as with a veil. He argues that this rules out picturing the soul as an extended circle: interweaving signifies its animating presence through similitude, covering signifies its transcendence and separate comprehension. The soul revolves in itself vitally and intellectually, unlike the body, which revolves in place; this self-conversion is where Plato gives the soul its knowing character. Because a divine soul begins from its most divine energies rather than from the imperfect, its wise life is unceasing.","ideas":["Interweaving means animation and union, not physical contact","Covering as a veil means the soul is exempt from body while comprehending it","Conversion to itself is the soul's self-knowledge and knowledge of all things","Divine souls proceed from the perfect to the secondary; generated things from imperfect to perfect"],"terms":[{"t":"epistrophē (conversion)","d":"The return of a thing to its source or to itself; here the basis of the soul's knowledge."},{"t":"bios (life)","d":"Life as the evolved form of a choice, said properly of souls and only by extension of intellect."}]},{"i":1490,"j":1496,"loc":"Book III, on Tim. 36e (2 of 2)","title":"The world-soul lives through the whole of time","summary":"Proclus asks how the world-soul surpasses other divine souls, and answers through Plato's phrase \"through the whole of time\": it alone has the whole of time as the measure of its period. Supermundane souls grasp several intelligibles at once, whereas the world-soul is the first to grasp one at a time, which makes it mundane yet the highest of mundane souls. He explains that transition and time arise in the soul because its partible intelligence applies part after part of itself to fixed intellect. He also explains why the soul can live in time before Plato has described time's generation, and closes with a summary of the soul's guardian, vivific and demiurgic activities.","ideas":["The universe's restoration (apocatastasis) coincides with the soul's intellective period","Soul's transitive thought stems from its partible essence circling the impartible","The soul co-produces animal time with the Demiurgus, being self-moved"],"terms":[{"t":"apocatastasis","d":"The return of a moving thing to its original position, completing a period."}]},{"i":1497,"j":1503,"loc":"Book III, on Tim. 36e–37a","title":"Visible body, invisible soul, best of generated things","summary":"On the lemma calling the soul invisible and the best of generated things, Proclus stresses the gap between soul and body despite the shared terms Plato applies to both. The soul is a middle nature: last of eternal beings, first of generated ones, eternal in a way yet acting in time. Body is the most beautiful generated thing, soul the best, as beauty is secondary to the good. He rejects reading the passage as making the Demiurgus contain the intelligibles as forms, and explains that harmony and rational energy exist primarily in the Demiurgus (Apollonian and Mercurial) and in the soul by participation, with threefold grades of harmony, reason, essence, figure and power.","ideas":["Soul is twofold-perpetual, between eternity and generation","Most beautiful (body) ranks below best (soul)","Harmony itself belongs to intellect, the first harmonized to soul, the secondarily harmonized to body"],"terms":[{"t":"Mousagetes","d":"Leader of the Muses, Apollo, the source of harmony within the Demiurgus."}]},{"i":1504,"j":1513,"loc":"Book III, on Tim. 37a–b (1 of 2)","title":"How the soul knows by contact","summary":"Proclus turns from the soul's motive to its gnostic energies. Plato calls its knowing a contact with partible and impartible essence; the soul knows by its own essence, sameness, difference and harmony whatever corresponds in things, as Empedocles said like is known by like. A single reason, prior to the two circles, knows both intelligibles and sensibles and can say they differ. He reviews Porphyry's report that Amelius read \"it ends\" instead of \"it speaks\", prefers \"speaks\", but offers a sense in which \"ends\" could stand.","ideas":["Soul is image of what is above it and paradigm of what is below","Knowledge requires likeness through a shared reason or form","One essential reason underlies the circle of the same and the circle of the different","Textual dispute: legei (speaks) versus lēgei (ends)"],"terms":[{"t":"logos (reason)","d":"The soul's one gnostic power; to speak is its proper activity."},{"t":"circle of the same / of the different","d":"The two circles of the soul, inclining respectively toward intelligible and sensible being."}]},{"i":1514,"j":1518,"loc":"Book III, on Tim. 37a–b (2 of 2)","title":"What the soul asserts about same and different","summary":"Proclus sets out three ways of punctuating the lemma, as one, two or three sentences, then turns to substance. He shows how relation, situation, quality, \"when\" and passion can be found analogously among intelligibles, not only sensibles: relation as paternal and maternal, \"how\" as different degrees of participation, suffering as participation, as in the Sophista. The world-soul speaks of all of these through one knowledge. He compares it to a tragic poet who holds the single cause of every speech in a drama, and to a many-headed animal speaking Greek and barbarian tongues.","ideas":["Categories have intelligible analogues fitted to essences","Plato uses \"suffering\" to signify participation","The cosmos is like one drama whose author is the world-soul"],"terms":[]},{"i":1519,"j":1529,"loc":"Book III, on Tim. 37b","title":"Reason becoming true and right opinion","summary":"Proclus records that Atticus took \"reason\" to be the soul's attentive power, Porphyry the charioteer driving two horses, Iamblichus the whole soul; he finds Porphyry most fitting. This reason is a single power of the soul's one essence that knows intelligibles and sensibles at once, \"becoming\" rather than simply being true because its knowledge is transitive. On the next lemma, when the circle of the different runs straight and reports sensibles, stable and true opinions and belief arise. He explains why the same circle knows intelligibles and the different sensibles, and insists the world-soul needs no organs, knowing sensibles from its own reasons.","ideas":["Inward reason moves without sound or voice","The circle of the different moving with rectitude yields opinion and faith","The world-soul knows sensibles causally, not by receiving impressions"],"terms":[{"t":"doxa (opinion)","d":"Knowledge belonging to the doxastic part of the soul; faith (pistis) is its stable judgment."}]},{"i":1530,"j":1539,"loc":"Book III, on Tim. 37c","title":"Intellect, science, and the close of the psychogony","summary":"When reason turns to the rational object and the circle of the same reports it, intellect and science arise. Proclus reads the \"logistic\" as the intelligible itself, and takes this intellect as one existing by habit in the soul, against Iamblichus, who made it an intellect prior to soul. He then reads the following lemma: whatever receives intellect, science, opinion and faith is soul. He closes the psychogony with a recapitulation (essence, harmony, figure, powers, energies), links the Demiurgus' acts to several demiurgic gods, and derives the three means from the daughters of Themis: Eunomia, Dice and Irene.","ideas":["Intellect has three grades: divine, participated, and by habit","Science knows through causes; intellect by simple intuition","Geometric, arithmetic and harmonic means organize soul and cosmos"],"terms":[{"t":"epistēmē (science)","d":"Knowledge from causes that composes and divides forms."}]},{"i":1540,"j":1551,"loc":"Book IV, on Tim. 37c–d","title":"The father delighted with the moving statue","summary":"Book IV opens on the lemma where the father sees the living image of the perpetual Gods and, delighted, makes it more like its paradigm. Proclus discusses the Demiurgus' generative, perfecting, assimilating and guardian powers, and notes that time is a higher gift than soul. The \"perpetual Gods\" are the intelligible Gods between animal itself and the Demiurgus, and the world is their statue, filled with deity throughout. He compares the Demiurgus to a telestic priest animating statues, takes his delight as joy in his own power, and names three causes of receiving good: the giver's power, the receiver's aptitude, and their symmetry.","ideas":["The world is an agalma (statue) of the intelligible Gods","Time is an eighth gift, greater than the soul that preceded it","Divine goods are received only by those fitted to receive them"],"terms":[{"t":"agalma (statue)","d":"Cult image; Proclus connects it with agallesthai, the god's delight."},{"t":"telestai","d":"Ritual initiators who animate statues through characters and names."}]},{"i":1552,"j":1567,"loc":"Book IV, on Tim. 37d (1 of 4)","title":"What eternity is","summary":"On the lemma of time as a moving image of eternity, Proclus reviews popular and Aristotelian views of time and eternity. He argues that eternity ranks above animal itself, since animal itself is eternal by participation; eternity is not one of the genera of being nor their sum, and nothing is opposed to it. Eternity is the comprehension of the intelligible unities, proximately above animal itself, and is called father-begotten light in the Chaldean Oracles. Interpreting \"abiding in one\", he rejects placing eternity in the Good and, with his teacher, sets it in the one being, as a duad of \"always\" and \"being\", against Strato.","ideas":["As eternity is to animal itself, so time is to the world","Eternity is not an animal, nor a genus like permanency or sameness","Sequence: one being, eternity, the eternal"],"terms":[{"t":"aiōn (eternity)","d":"Taken as aei ōn, \"always being\": the cause of permanency in existence."}]},{"i":1568,"j":1575,"loc":"Book IV, on Tim. 37d (2 of 4)","title":"Time as moving image proceeding by number","summary":"Proclus explains why Plato says \"was eternal\", and why the eternal cannot be fully fitted to the generated, though eternity is present to the world in a way. Time and eternity are measures, the one of mundane, the other of intelligible beings: eternity as unity, time as number. Time is moveable not as a whole; its monad stays with the Demiurgus while it proceeds by intellectual number. It measures not as we measure but by producing and defining each life-span. Against those who call time a cause of decay, he cites theurgists who worship time as a god, older and younger, infinite in power, spiral in form.","ideas":["Time's production is the Demiurgus' \"contrivance\"","Something of time abides while it proceeds","Time sets each being's span: an ox's, a man's, the sun's period"],"terms":[{"t":"epinoia (contrivance)","d":"Plato's word for the Demiurgus' devising of time."}]},{"i":1576,"j":1587,"loc":"Book IV, on Tim. 37d (3 of 4)","title":"Time is not the offspring of soul","summary":"Proclus rejects views that make time a mere concept, an accident, or a product of the world-soul's transitive activity. Plato has the Demiurgus make time after soul and body are complete, and soul is perfected by time, so it cannot be its cause; inanimate things too share in time. Time is an essence, not an accident, and superior to soul. Rejecting Theodorus' identification of time and eternity with the soul's two circles, he defines time as an intellect that abides in its inward energy and moves in what it gives. He derives chronos from a \"dancing intellect\".","ideas":["Strongest argument: no cause needs its own offspring to be perfected","Time is a mean between the wholly immoveable and the self-moved","Eternal by inward energy, moveable in its participants"],"terms":[{"t":"chronos (time)","d":"Etymologized as choreuōn nous, an intellect that dances."}]},{"i":1588,"j":1596,"loc":"Book IV, on Tim. 37d (4 of 4)","title":"The causes and motion of time","summary":"Proclus sets out the causes of time: the Demiurgus as maker, eternity as paradigm, and the circular return of moving things to one as end. Time's own motion is pure, even progression, unlike the motions of soul, nature and body, and is measured only by the temporal monad, the Demiurgus and eternity. It is called moveable only relative to eternity, as soul is called partible relative to intellect. Further arguments follow: the Seasons, Month, Day and Night are worshipped as gods, so time is all the more a god. With Iamblichus he calls time the first of images, and praises Iamblichus over Porphyry, Amelius and Numenius.","ideas":["Periodic return lets moving things imitate abiding in one","Time's order is arranging, not arranged (Iamblichus)","Images exist in middle as well as last beings"],"terms":[]},{"i":1597,"j":1607,"loc":"Book IV, on Tim. 37e","title":"Days, months, years, and the species of time","summary":"On the lemma naming days, nights, months and years as parts of time and \"was\" and \"will be\" as its species, Proclus rejects defining day as lit air or as the sun's passage. Following Syrianus, he treats day, night, month and year as divine demiurgic measures prior to their visible forms. \"Was\" and \"will be\" came into being with the universe, so Atticus is wrong to posit a disorderly time and motion before the cosmos. He explains how the stars cooperate with time, serve as its instruments, and guard its numbers, and cites theurgic and Phrygian rites honouring time, Month (as Sabazius), and the Seasons.","ideas":["Parts of time are presided over by divine orders; species run through all of them","No motion, hence no time, before the cosmos (against Atticus)","Visible measures depend on invisible immovable causes"],"terms":[{"t":"Sabazius","d":"Phrygian god under whom, Proclus says, Month was celebrated."}]},{"i":1608,"j":1623,"loc":"Book IV, on Tim. 37e–38a","title":"Only \"is\" belongs to eternal being","summary":"Plato says we wrongly, through forgetfulness, apply \"was\" and \"will be\" to eternal essence, to which only \"is\" belongs. Proclus calls this misuse a kind of war of the Giants, and distinguishes the eternal \"is\" from the temporal \"is\" set with past and future. The cause of the error is the soul's forgetting after the loss of its wings. He lists the theurgists' grades of time (zonic, azonic, archangelic, ruling, fontal from Rhea), and with Iamblichus discusses the three ways generation is tied to time, distinguishing simple temporal species from what is measured in them.","ideas":["Two senses of \"is\": eternal and co-ordinated with time","Error arises from confusing image and paradigm","Intelligibles have older and younger without temporal becoming"],"terms":[]},{"i":1624,"j":1630,"loc":"Book IV, on Tim. 38a–c","title":"Time born with the universe","summary":"Proclus reads Plato's reproach of saying that what has come to be \"is\" become, and that non-being \"is\". The opposite error to the last lemma, it brings to generation words fitting eternal being; calling non-being \"is\" is worse. The fuller treatment Plato defers is, with Iamblichus, theological (Sophista, Parmenides) rather than logical. On the next lemma, time was generated with the universe so that they would be dissolved together, which Proclus takes to show that the world is unbegotten in time and indissoluble. Time and universe exist for each other and for the whole fabrication, and Plato next turns to the time displayed in the heavens.","ideas":["What is generated with time is not generated in time","The conditional \"if ever dissolved\" signals impossibility","The universe has was, is and will be through all of time"],"terms":[]},{"i":1631,"j":1639,"loc":"Book IV, on Tim. 38c","title":"Planets made to distinguish and guard time","summary":"The sun, moon and five planets were made to distinguish and guard the numbers of time. Proclus separates a supermundane time, produced by the Demiurgus' intellect, from a secondary visible time produced by his reason and discursive thought and unfolded through the planets, a ninth gift. The sun, called \"time of time\", holds the ruling rank, the moon the second. Planets seem to wander through advance, retreat and station, but their irregularity is orderly; he doubts epicycles. He lays out a monad and triad of temporal measures, explains the planets as world-rulers leading multitudes, and argues that time is prior to soul though participated in a generated way.","ideas":["Two times: one abiding and proceeding, one borne along in motion","Planetary motion is \"equably anomalous\"","One world-time common to all, with particular numbers for each being"],"terms":[{"t":"kosmokratores (world-rulers)","d":"The seven planets, which hold a total power in the cosmos."}]},{"i":1640,"j":1647,"loc":"Book IV, on Tim. 38c–d","title":"Planetary bodies and the order of the spheres","summary":"The Demiurgus made the bodies of the seven stars and set them in the seven circulations of the circle of the different. Proclus notes that Plato says he made the bodies \"of\" them, the stars being souls and intellects, and with Iamblichus takes the circulations as their intellectual souls, then suggests the powers of the whole soul. On the next lemma, placing the moon first and the sun second, he supports the order with the Republic, Orphic verse and the Chaldean Oracles. He weighs the mathematicians' central sun, finds their arguments from Venus and Mercury unproven, and notes Ptolemy's caution. He traces the moon-then-sun order to Anaxagoras.","ideas":["Sun and moon are father and mother of visible generation","The circle of the different stays one though cut into seven","Mathematical arguments from probability carry little weight"],"terms":[]},{"i":1648,"j":1654,"loc":"Book IV, on Tim. 38d–e","title":"Sun, Venus, Mercury and the planetary triads","summary":"Proclus asks why the sun, Venus and Mercury share one speed. He reports the mathematicians' linked epicycles and the scheme of Porphyry and Theodorus, which grades the planets by essence, life and intellect; Iamblichus rejects both and gives a simpler account: Venus and Mercury are solar, co-working with the sun. Their \"contrary power\" is explained by their stations and by the analogy of truth, beauty and symmetry. Mars, Jupiter and Saturn cause separation, symmetry and connection, and each planet governs a power of the irrational soul. Plato leaves the other stars aside, and Proclus asks where the soul of the fixed sphere is placed, citing the Republic's Sirens.","ideas":["Equal speed reflects shared demiurgic work","Planets mutually comprehend one another as their speeds vary","Grades of animation: universal, universal-partial, partial-universal, partial"],"terms":[]},{"i":1655,"j":1660,"loc":"Book IV, on Tim. 38e–39a","title":"Stars become animals; the oblique circle","summary":"Once the stars had their motions and were bound with living bonds, they became animals and learned their order. Proclus distinguishes each star's separable divine soul, by which it is a god, from the animated body by which it is an animal, citing the Phaedrus. Above both stand the god's unity and its intellect. The stars co-produce the parts of time while the father makes its whole. On the next lemma, the circle of the different moves obliquely along the zodiac and causes the variety of generation, while the circle of the same rules it and gives stability.","ideas":["Unity, intellect and soul: god, most divine, divine","\"Straight\" describes the soul's circle, \"oblique\" the bodily one","Dominance of sameness keeps the world one and perpetual"],"terms":[]},{"i":1661,"j":1670,"loc":"Book IV, on Tim. 39a–b","title":"Swiftness, slowness and the spiral motion","summary":"On the lemma that planets in smaller circles move faster and Saturn slower, Proclus works out geometrically how speed depends on the ratio of circle to circle against time to time. Planets turn about their own centres and are carried by their spheres, needing no astronomers' hypotheses. On the next lemma he explains why the faster planets appear to be overtaken, citing the Laws on knowing the true racers, with the example of the moon and Saturn near Cor Leonis. The spiral comes from motion in latitude and depth combined with the daily turning. Contrariety in the heavens is non-destructive, and the spiral suits planets as a mean between circle and straight line.","ideas":["Apparent and proper motions differ","Spiral mixes circle (fixed stars) and straight line (generation)","Heavenly contraries co-operate rather than destroy"],"terms":[{"t":"in consequentia","d":"The planets' proper eastward motion, against the daily westward turn."}]},{"i":1671,"j":1677,"loc":"Book IV, on Tim. 39b–c","title":"The sun kindled as a measure","summary":"The god kindled the sun's light in the second circle so that there would be a clear measure and the heaven be visible. Proclus makes the sun the ruling cause of visible time: its regular period makes it a measure of measures, and its light makes all periods known. Light is not for measurement's sake; it reveals total time and fills souls. He sees a twofold generation of the sun, as a planet and as supermundane king analogous to the Good, citing Orpheus' Phanes and the Chaldean solar world. He explains why the sun is not the first circle (its brightness is mediated by the moon) and calls the number learned from it doxastic.","ideas":["Sun as both mundane planet and supermundane leader","Only the sun in the cosmos is shadowless","Moon adapts solar light to generation"],"terms":[]},{"i":1678,"j":1690,"loc":"Book IV, on Tim. 39c–d","title":"Night, day, month, year, perfect year","summary":"Night and day are the period of the \"most wise circulation\", the fixed sphere; month and year follow the moon and sun. Proclus answers Aristotle's charge that Plato equates time with circulation: \"period\" means a measure of motion, not motion. He distinguishes the apparent daily return of the fixed sphere from its true return, and posits invisible, divine Night, Day, Month and Year, to be honoured in cult. On the next lemma the perfect year arrives when all eight periods return together. It is to be grasped as one number and not by adding years; Cancer is called the world's horoscope, and this number is distinct from the Republic's.","ideas":["Night is named first, as prior in intelligible causes","Planetary wanderings are \"inerratically erratic\"","The perfect number is a whole imitating eternity"],"terms":[{"t":"nycthemeron","d":"The span of a night and a day, the smallest measure of time."}]},{"i":1691,"j":1699,"loc":"Book IV, on Tim. 39d–e","title":"Time's completion and the third wholeness","summary":"Proclus completes the account of time: like soul, it is both eternal and generated, a monad and centre in itself, number and circle as it proceeds. He sets Plato against the Stoics, who made time a mere concept, and the Peripatetics, who made it an accident of motion. Planets move about their centres and through their spheres, all concentric, without epicycles. On the next lemma, the universe still lacked the animals within it. He sees Plato giving the world three likenesses to animal itself (only-begotten, perpetual, all-perfect) and three wholenesses, the last, a whole in each part, being the tenth gift.","ideas":["Time has a triad and heptad of derived times","Epicycles are fitting hypotheses for reason but foreign to nature","Wholes before parts, of parts, and in a part"],"terms":[]},{"i":1700,"j":1707,"loc":"Book IV, on Tim. 39e","title":"Animal itself and the demiurgic intellect","summary":"On the lemma that intellect saw the ideas in animal itself and judged the universe must contain as many, Proclus explains how every paternal order keeps some causes within and sends others forth, as with Heaven, Saturn and the Demiurgus. Animal itself is intelligible intellect, third after intelligible being and life (eternity), the Orphic Phanes. The Demiurgus is intellectual intellect who \"sees\" it within himself, not outside. Forms are finite, not infinite. He criticizes Amelius' triad of demiurgic intellects and Numenius' three gods, following Iamblichus' refutation.","ideas":["Intelligible triad: being, life, intellect","The Demiurgus' vision is union; his dianoia proceeds to making","\"Such and so many\" mark the character and number of ideas"],"terms":[{"t":"Phanes","d":"Orphic god identified with animal itself, revealing the intelligible unities."}]},{"i":1708,"j":1717,"loc":"Book IV, on Tim. 39e–40a","title":"The four ideas of living beings","summary":"Four ideas: celestial gods, winged air-dwellers, water creatures, land creatures. Proclus treats animal itself as monad, duad (male and female) and tetrad, citing a Pythagorean hymn on the tetrad and decad. Following Syrianus, each idea covers every genus in its region: gods, daemons and mortals alike. He explains the names at each level, and argues the Demiurgus, unlike animal itself, holds separate ideas of mortal beings. Fire is not named because it truly belongs to the heavens; sublunary fire is an efflux of it, as the Chaldeans and Aristotle suggest.","ideas":["Ideas divide into a monad and triad, or two genera and two species","Fewer, more total forms higher; more, more partial ones lower","Fire's proper place is the celestial region"],"terms":[{"t":"tetractys / decad","d":"Pythagorean numbers: the divine tetrad proceeding from the monad and filling the decad, here the world."}]},{"i":1718,"j":1729,"loc":"Book IV, on Tim. 40a","title":"Divine fire and the round stars","summary":"Proclus closes his discussion of the four elements: true fire, which is pure light, is in the heavens, the most attenuated sublunary fire lies at the top of the air, and the grossest fire is in the earth. He then takes up the lemma saying the Demiurgus made the divine kind mostly of fire and round. Weighing the interpreters, he concludes that all four elements are in the heavens, but immaterially and only as their summits, with fire ruling because it plays the part of form. Against critics who say fire tends upward while stars move in circles, he answers that upward motion belongs only to fire out of its proper place. The stars are round because they are likened to the universe, a whole that exists before its parts.","ideas":["All elements are in the heavens as summits, with fire predominating as form","Upward or downward motion belongs to bodies out of their natural place; in place they rest or revolve","Celestial fire is light, splendid and beautiful, unlike caustic sublunary fire","Parts can resemble the whole only where the whole exists before the parts"],"terms":[{"t":"summits of the elements","d":"The purest, highest forms of earth, water, air and fire, which Proclus places in the heavenly bodies."},{"t":"fifth body","d":"Aristotle's name for the heavenly substance, which Proclus says tells us only that it differs from the four elements."}]},{"i":1730,"j":1735,"loc":"Book IV, on Tim. 40a–b","title":"Stars set in the wisdom of the same","summary":"The lemma says the Demiurgus placed the stars in the wisdom of the best and most powerful, spread round the whole heaven. Proclus reads this as placing them in the soul of the inerratic sphere, so that each star has its own intellectual soul as well as the soul of the universe. He argues this from the fact that higher animals have their own souls besides the world soul, and from Aristotle's principle that each perpetual motion needs its own mover. The inerratic sphere is a monad over the multitude of fixed stars, while each planetary sphere has both a wholeness and leaders. Iamblichus places this wisdom in the paradigm, and Proclus ends by matching the regions with Eunomia, Dice and Irene among the Seasons, and Aglaïa, Thalia and Euphrosyne among the Graces.","ideas":["Each star has its own divine, intellectual soul as well as the world soul","Orderly motion shows the star's soul is intellectual","The inerratic sphere is a co-arranged monad leading the multitude of fixed stars"],"terms":[{"t":"circle of the same","d":"The outer circuit of the world soul, carrying the fixed stars and ruling all the other revolutions."},{"t":"cosmocrators","d":"The world-ruling leaders, here the planets, who lead the multitude in each celestial sphere."}]},{"i":1736,"j":1746,"loc":"Book IV, on Tim. 40b (1 of 2)","title":"The two motions of the fixed stars","summary":"Commenting on the two motions given to each star, Proclus argues that both the star's soul and its body move in two ways. The soul thinks the same things in the same way and also follows the circuit of the same; the body spins on its own centre and is carried forward with the whole sphere. He explains how Plato's words should be punctuated to fit this reading, and says that contraries can coexist in the heavens because they are free of sublunary matter. The fixed stars are spared the other five motions because they are better than them, not because they lack something. He ends by rejecting the motion of the fixed stars about the poles of the zodiac taught by Ptolemy and Hipparchus, citing the Chaldean Oracles, the theurgist Julian, the Bear and Canobus, and the point that a hypothesis matching the phenomena need not be true.","ideas":["Soul and body of each star each have a twofold motion","The universe moves uniformly, the fixed stars in two ways, sublunary bodies in many","Freedom from the five motions is a superior power, not a privation","Proclus rejects the precession of the fixed stars"],"terms":[{"t":"advancing motion","d":"The forward motion of a star carried from place to place with its sphere, as distinct from rotation on its centre."},{"t":"motion in consequentia","d":"The slow motion of the fixed stars about the zodiac's poles (precession), which Proclus denies."}]},{"i":1747,"j":1760,"loc":"Book IV, on Tim. 40b (2 of 2)","title":"Fixed stars and planets as divine animals","summary":"On the lemma calling the fixed stars divine animals that always revolve in the same, Proclus says this implies each has a divine soul and an intellect hung from a god, and that they neither got their souls at a certain time nor will lose them. The planets are recalled after the fixed stars because their varied motions are secondary; Plato makes them move nearer and farther from earth by their own motion, not through epicycles. All heaven is made of all elements, with fire ruling everywhere, the stars being more solid than their spheres. Answering Aristotle's question why one sphere holds many stars but each planetary sphere one, Proclus says each planet leads a whole invisible world of gods, as with Hecate and Diana in the Moon and Bacchus in the Sun. He concludes that the fixed stars outrank the planets in dignity as well as place.","ideas":["Divine animals have a soul, an intellect and a god on which they depend","Planets move through the depth of their spheres by their own nature","Each planet leads an invisible multitude of gods and dæmons","The fixed stars live by the circle of the same, the planets more by the circle of the different"],"terms":[{"t":"apocatastasis","d":"The return of a heavenly body to its starting position, completing a period."},{"t":"azonic","d":"A rank of gods not bound to particular zones, which theologists assign within the cosmocrators."}]},{"i":1761,"j":1768,"loc":"Book IV, on Tim. 40b–c","title":"Earth our nurse, conglobed about the pole","summary":"The lemma calls Earth our nurse, conglobed about the pole, guardian and maker of night and day, and the first and most ancient of the gods within the heavens. Proclus explains why Earth is discussed beside the stars: they move about it, and it is a mother to heaven's father in the generation of meteors. Earth is a divine animal with intellect, soul, ethereal body and visible bulk, and it nourishes our souls and intellects as well as our bodies. He argues that the Greek word for conglobed means gathered, not moved, so Plato keeps the Earth still, unlike Heraclides Ponticus; the axis is a holding power, called Atlas. Earth makes night with its cone of shadow and, with the Sun, makes day, and it contains terrestrial forms of Ceres, Vesta, Isis and other gods.","ideas":["Earth is a living divine animal with intellect and soul, not just a mass","Plato's word for 'conglobed' means compressed, so the Earth is unmoved","Earth makes night by its shadow and guards the balance of night and day","All celestial gods have terrestrial counterparts in the Earth"],"terms":[{"t":"illomenēn (conglobed)","d":"Plato's word for the Earth, which Proclus reads as gathered together, against Aristotle's reading as moving."},{"t":"axis","d":"The pole extended through the universe, read as a single divine power that holds the world together."}]},{"i":1769,"j":1775,"loc":"Book IV, on Tim. 40c","title":"Why Earth is first and most ancient","summary":"Proclus defends calling Earth the first and most ancient of the gods within the heavens against those who see only its dark bulk. He points to its stability, generative power, concord with heaven and central position, which the Pythagoreans called the tower of Jupiter, and to the Phædo's true Earth, a twelve-skinned sphere with rivers of every element. Dignity, he says, is judged from powers rather than place, and Earth has every mark of the divine orders. By conversion the centre is joined to the poles, and the unmoved is older than what is moved in generation. Earth also copies the intellectual Earth as nurse, guard and Titanic power, and the word 'machinated' shows the skill needed for Earth and Sun to produce night and day together.","ideas":["Earth holds within itself all that heaven holds, but in an earthly way","Rank depends on power and essence, not on location","The centre and the poles are joined in one circle of conversion","Night and day need the Earth, the Sun and the sphere together"],"terms":[{"t":"tower of Jupiter","d":"Pythagorean name for the centre of the universe, which holds a demiurgic guard."}]},{"i":1776,"j":1785,"loc":"Book IV, on Tim. 40c–d","title":"Dances of the stars and the visible Gods","summary":"Plato declines to explain the stars' dances, conjunctions, occultations and omens without models, and closes his account of the visible gods. Proclus says this is because astronomy differs from physiology and needs instruments and observation. Read mathematically, the lemma covers the stars' circuits, joint risings, direct and retrograde motions, conjunctions and eclipses; Proclus calls epicycles and eccentrics unworthy of divine bodies, yet useful devices for breaking varied motions into simple ones. Read philosophically, the dances are the souls' circuits about the intelligible, and their conjunctions are unions with intellect. He cites Theophrastus on Chaldean prediction from the heavens, and explains that these gods are visible because they have bodies and generated because they have soul.","ideas":["Astronomy needs instruments and observation, which physiology does not supply","Epicycles and eccentrics are tools of calculation, not real structures","Each astronomical term has a parallel in the life of souls","The heavens signify past as well as future events"],"terms":[{"t":"epicycles and eccentrics","d":"Astronomers' devices for analysing planetary motion, which Proclus treats as hypotheses rather than physical facts."},{"t":"synod","d":"A conjunction of heavenly bodies."}]},{"i":1786,"j":1801,"loc":"Book IV, on Tim. 40d–e","title":"Sublunary Gods and trust in ancient men","summary":"Plato says that knowing the generation of the other dæmons is beyond us and that we must trust the children of the gods. Proclus explains why the sublunary gods are harder to speak of: physiologists, Aristotle among them, left generation without providence, and souls forget what is near more readily than what is high. He argues these powers are gods, called dæmons only relative to the celestial gods, and that the Demiurgus would not leave the elements without gods when telestic art can bring the divine into statues. They come after the celestial gods, from Heaven and Earth, and he takes up how irrational dæmons can be both immortal and from the junior gods. The 'children of the gods' are souls who know their leading god and have inspired knowledge, so Timæus follows the Orphic tradition handed on by Pythagoras.","ideas":["The sublunary region too is governed by gods and dæmons","Plato is silent as a physiologist because the phenomena give no clear evidence","Some junior gods generate others, so not all their offspring are mortal","Inspired knowledge comes through kinship with the gods"],"terms":[{"t":"genesiurgic Gods","d":"The gods who produce and govern generation in the sublunary region."},{"t":"telestic art","d":"The art of mystic rites, which makes statues and places fit to receive the gods."}]},{"i":1802,"j":1811,"loc":"Book V, introduction","title":"The golden chain and the sublunary Gods","summary":"Book V opens with the sublunary gods as the last link of a golden chain reaching from the intelligible gods down through the intellectual, supercelestial and celestial orders. These gods are intellectual in essence but mundane in their allotments, govern generation without being generated, and use bodies simpler than the elements. Proclus sets out a triad of angels, dæmons and heroes between gods and men, linked to being, life and intellect, each group taking the name and character of its leading god. He sets aside earlier readings of the gods of the lemma as fables, city founders or souls, and refers Timæus to the Orphic kingdoms of Phanes, Night, Heaven, Saturn, Jupiter and Bacchus. Plato starts from Heaven and Earth because the Greeks knew Hesiod's theogony better.","ideas":["The chain of gods runs from the intelligible down to the sublunary","Angels, dæmons and heroes fill the gap between gods and men","Followers bear the names of the gods they depend on","Each divine kingdom has an analogue in every element"],"terms":[{"t":"golden chain","d":"The continuous series of divine orders from the intelligible gods to the sublunary deities."},{"t":"angelic, dæmoniacal, heroic","d":"The three kinds of superior beings, linked to being, life and intellectual conversion."}]},{"i":1812,"j":1826,"loc":"Book V, on Tim. 40e (1 of 2)","title":"Ocean and Tethys from Heaven and Earth","summary":"On the lemma that Ocean and Tethys were born of Heaven and Earth, Proclus makes Heaven the father who bounds and Earth the mother who multiplies, present in a fitting way in every element. He lists the interpreters' readings, including Iamblichus's and Theodorus's 'life according to habitude', and then follows Syrianus. Heaven's union with Earth is a marriage, as in Orpheus and the Athenian rites, and produces Ocean and Tethys, who abide in their parents and also go forth. Ocean causes all motion, intellectual, psychic and physical, in each element; Tethys keeps each stream of motion pure and in its place, as her name suggests in the Cratylus. Proclus ends by explaining why these gods come from Heaven and Earth rather than from the world soul.","ideas":["Heaven gives bound, Earth gives infinity and generative power","Divine generation is a union of powers, called marriage, not physical coupling","Ocean is the source of motion, Tethys separates and settles it","The world soul works first in heaven and through it in the sublunary realm"],"terms":[{"t":"Ocean","d":"For Proclus the divine cause of all motion and progression in secondary natures."},{"t":"Tethys","d":"The goddess who separates the streams of motion and gives each a pure, settled place."},{"t":"habitude","d":"Theodorus's term for a life or soul held in relation to a body."}]},{"i":1827,"j":1841,"loc":"Book V, on Tim. 40e (2 of 2)","title":"Phorcys, Saturn and Rhea","summary":"On the next lemma, which derives Phorcys, Saturn and Rhea from Ocean and Tethys, Proclus first faces a conflict with Orpheus, who makes Ocean and Tethys siblings of Saturn and Rhea. He answers that elder siblings can be called parents, as Jupiter is called father by Juno and Neptune in Homer, and recounts the Orphic story of Ocean staying home when the other Titans attacked Heaven. In the sublunary realm Ocean comes before Saturn because flux and motion lead there. He reports the views of Theodorus and Iamblichus, who place the three gods in spheres or regions. His own view is that Phorcys rules seminal principles in nature, Saturn divides forms intellectually, and Rhea draws them out into life.","ideas":["A god can be both brother and father of another","Ocean abides with Heaven while the other Titans move away","Saturn divides, Rhea gives life, Phorcys handles seminal principles","'Such as subsist with these' takes in the other Titans and their followers"],"terms":[{"t":"Titans","d":"The children of Heaven and Earth; for Proclus the dividing, separating order of gods."},{"t":"spermatic productive principles","d":"The seed-like forming principles in nature, over which Phorcys presides."}]},{"i":1842,"j":1858,"loc":"Book V, on Tim. 41a (1 of 2)","title":"Jupiter, Juno and the sublunary ennead","summary":"The lemma derives Jupiter, Juno and their brothers and children from Saturn and Rhea. After the views of Iamblichus and Theodorus, Proclus says there are several Jupiters; this one brings Saturn's hidden divisions into visible things, while Juno moves and unfolds the forms that are generated. Their brothers rule air, water and earth, and their nameless children carry out the partial fabrication. The nine gods together complete the sublunary world, and Proclus explains why the number nine, the three pairs and the tetrad fit generation. On the next lemma, he says the gods who become visible when they please must have bodies finer than the elements, and that they are unmixed with matter, against both the Stoics and Numenius.","ideas":["Heaven bounds, Earth strengthens, Ocean moves, Tethys settles, Saturn divides, Rhea vivifies, Phorcys sows, Jupiter perfects, Juno unfolds","The ennead fits generation, which runs out without returning","Sublunary gods use invisible vehicles finer than the elements","The gods govern mixed things without being mixed with matter"],"terms":[{"t":"ennead","d":"The nine sublunary gods from Heaven and Earth to Jupiter and Juno, which together complete the world of generation."}]},{"i":1859,"j":1871,"loc":"Book V, on Tim. 41a (2 of 2)","title":"The Demiurgus's speech and 'Gods of Gods'","summary":"Proclus explains that the Demiurgus's words are neither spoken, physical, psychic nor merely intellectual, but demiurgic powers that make their hearers into makers. He praises the speech's grand and concise style, and compares Homer's Jupiter, who gathers the gods either directly or through Themis, holding that Plato's single speech contains both modes. He rejects readings of 'Gods of Gods' that join it to the next words, make the gods images of intelligibles, or limit it to the leading gods. Following Syrianus, he says each mundane god is an invisible deity joined to a visible god, its body. The speech is addressed to the compound of divine soul and animal, which receives the will of the father through reason.","ideas":["The father's words pass powers to the gods and make them demiurgic","The gods remain unmixed by imitating the father and provide by fabricating","'Gods of Gods' means invisible deities joined to visible ones","The speech is addressed to souls with their bodies, not to bare intellects"],"terms":[{"t":"Gods of Gods","d":"The address to the mundane gods, read as gods who are themselves taken part in by visible divine bodies."},{"t":"Themis","d":"In Homer the goddess through whom Jupiter calls the gods to council, holder of the divine laws of providence."}]},{"i":1872,"j":1886,"loc":"Book V, on Tim. 41a–b","title":"Indissoluble by the father's will","summary":"On 'whatever is generated by me is indissoluble', Proclus sees three acts of the Demiurgus, which make the gods divine, connected and like the paradigm, and he links them to the three intelligible triads. He distinguishes father, maker, father-and-maker and maker-and-father, placing Jupiter as maker and father. He then analyses bonds: whatever is bound is in some way dissoluble, and four grades run from the simply indissoluble intelligibles to mortal things. The mundane gods are in between, dissoluble only because their causes are held in the father, and indissoluble by nature and by his will. Against Plutarch, Atticus and Severus, he argues they are not corruptible by nature and kept only by will; since only the binder could undo the world and that would be evil, it is incorruptible.","ideas":["A bond keeps many things united while each stays itself","Four grades of dissolubility, with the mundane gods in the middle","The Demiurgus's will joins power and goodness and is the strongest bond","The world cannot be dissolved, since only its good maker could undo it"],"terms":[{"t":"bond (desmos)","d":"What holds many distinct things together in one, which always implies a matching mode of dissolution."}]},{"i":1887,"j":1902,"loc":"Book V, on Tim. 41b","title":"Not immortal, yet never to die","summary":"On 'so far as you are generated you are not immortal', Proclus explains how the mundane gods sit between the extremes. What is primarily immortal gives itself life, mortal things lack lasting life, and the mundane gods receive endless life from another. He ranks kinds of immortality: the eternal, that of the mundane gods, that of dæmons who go down and come back without forgetting, and that of partial souls who forget and keep only their essential life. He distinguishes four kinds of death, from which the gods and essential dæmons are wholly free. The father's will is a bond of bonds, and the call 'learn now' fills the gods with intellectual conceptions from intelligible animal before they turn to provide for lower things.","ideas":["The mundane gods' immortality is received and always coming to be","Dæmons are the true middle between gods and partial souls","Four kinds of death apply to different beings","Learning the father's will comes before imitating his power"],"terms":[{"t":"homonymously immortal","d":"Immortal in name only, the immortality the Demiurgus grants to partial souls."}]},{"i":1903,"j":1913,"loc":"Book V, on Tim. 41b–c","title":"Three mortal kinds still to be made","summary":"The Demiurgus says three mortal kinds remain, without which the world would be incomplete, but that if he made them they would equal the gods. Proclus explains that the ideas of winged, aquatic and land animals in animal itself cause all such animals, but intellectual difference divides them for mortal things. Mortal kinds must exist so that everything possible exists, so that the divine is not the last of things, and so that the world copies its paradigm; he links this to the Orphic story of Proserpine's unfinished webs. What an unmoved cause produces alone is lasting; what it produces through moved causes lasts in form but dies as an individual. 'Through me' and 'participating of life' show that soul makes bodies divine, and 'equalised' means made similar, not equal.","ideas":["Mortal animals perish one by one but last as species","Mortal kinds are needed for the world to be complete","The Demiurgus makes eternal things through himself and mortal things through the junior gods","Soul is what deifies bodies"],"terms":[{"t":"animal itself","d":"The intelligible paradigm, which holds the ideas of all four kinds of living things."},{"t":"junior Gods","d":"The mundane gods to whom the Demiurgus hands the making of mortal natures."}]},{"i":1914,"j":1920,"loc":"Book V, on Tim. 41c (1 of 2)","title":"Turn yourselves to the making of animals","summary":"On the command to turn to making animals and imitate the father's power, Proclus sees two aims: to give mortals existence, which is providence, and to make the universe like its complete paradigm. 'Convert yourselves' rouses the gods, like Jupiter sending the gods to war in Homer, and applies above all to the planets and the Sun. 'According to nature' limits their work, places nature under them as a tool, and marks them as intermediaries who make the last things. There are no ideas of individuals like Socrates; the Demiurgus divides by kinds and makes mortal things in an immortal way. Imitating his power is the highest aim of the second fabrication, through which he passes on his will and power.","ideas":["The second fabrication has a providential and an assimilative aim","Intermediaries make the last things, as primary things make intermediaries","There are no ideas of individuals","The father gives will by saying what he wills and power by asking for imitation"],"terms":[{"t":"second fabrication","d":"The making of mortal natures by the junior gods, following the first fabrication of the Demiurgus."}]},{"i":1921,"j":1931,"loc":"Book V, on Tim. 41c (2 of 2)","title":"The seed of what is divine in mortals","summary":"The Demiurgus says he himself will provide the seed of the part of mortals that shares the name of the immortals. Proclus explains that the Demiurgus makes all divine animals and the rational life in mortals, and hands the rest to the junior gods, so that he is clearly the cause of everything immortal. He criticises Platonists who make our soul equal in essence to the gods or able to become the One, and sets out four grades from primarily immortal to mortal. The partial soul comes first from the Demiurgus, then from the world soul, and then from a partial intellect. Justice follows Jupiter, and the 'seed' means generation, the first of three sowings, which includes making the soul's vehicle.","ideas":["The rational soul is called divine but is not simply divine","A true cause is whole and of a higher order than its effect","Justice goes with the mundane gods in governing the universe","The Demiurgus begins the vehicle and the rational soul"],"terms":[{"t":"semination","d":"Sowing; Proclus reads it with Syrianus as the generation of souls, followed by their distribution to the gods and to generation."}]},{"i":1932,"j":1947,"loc":"Book V, on Tim. 41d (1 of 3)","title":"Weaving the mortal with the immortal","summary":"On the command to weave together the immortal and the mortal, Proclus surveys the interpreters: Atticus and Albinus let the irrational soul and vehicle perish, Porphyry has them dissolved into the spheres, and others make them immortal. He argues Plato keeps the irrational life after death, since souls are punished and choose lives in Hades and ride their vehicles there. With Syrianus he holds that the pneumatic vehicle keeps the summits of the irrational life forever, while the junior gods weave a longer but mortal life that is dropped after purification. He then explains the mandate to make, nourish and take back animals, which covers every moving animal, provides plants as food, and ends in regeneration. Proclus counts the powers the speech gave the gods, and says the words 'these things spake' mark the speech as complete and meant for souls.","ideas":["The vehicle and summit of irrational life last; the woven mortal life does not","Animals that move from place to place have rational, immortal souls","Taking back the corrupted renews generation","The speech gives the gods unifying, connective, knowing, perfecting and demiurgic powers"],"terms":[{"t":"vehicle (ochēma)","d":"The fine body the soul rides, given by the Demiurgus, distinct from the outward body."},{"t":"pneumatic vehicle","d":"The spirit-body that holds the summits of the irrational life."}]},{"i":1948,"j":1957,"loc":"Book V, on Tim. 41d (2 of 3)","title":"The Crater and partial souls","summary":"On the lemma where the Demiurgus pours the rest into the former Crater, Proclus says making and speaking are one act for the gods. 'Again' marks a more partial power of the Demiurgus, and partial souls come from the same Crater and genera as the world soul but in a lesser way. He rejects those who make our souls of the same essence as divine souls, against Theodorus. He reviews Theodorus's two Craters, which Atticus also found, and Iamblichus's view of one life-giving cause, and then follows Syrianus. On this view the Crater is a life-giving fountain of souls beside the Demiurgus, which Orpheus calls Juno. Plato names it only for partial souls because they separate from it, while divine souls remain in it.","ideas":["To think and to make are the same for the Demiurgus","Partial souls differ in essence from divine souls, not just in activity","The Crater is a fountain of soul distinct from the Demiurgus","Partial souls leave the Crater; divine souls stay in it"],"terms":[{"t":"Crater","d":"The mixing bowl of the Timæus, read as the fontal life-giving cause of souls, linked with Juno."},{"t":"partial souls","d":"Individual souls such as ours, which can descend into generation."}]},{"i":1958,"j":1967,"loc":"Book V, on Tim. 41d (3 of 3)","title":"Supermundane souls and a definition of soul","summary":"Proclus argues against Iamblichus that the soul built from the middle genera is the world soul, and asks whether Plato knew of supermundane souls. In what he calls his own oracle, he proposes souls that are more impartible than partible and do not reach as far as solid bodies, with harmonies beyond the three sensible genera. Partial souls have more divisions in their ratios. He offers a general definition of soul as an essence between true being and generation, against Aristotle's entelecheia, and explains why Plato names only the world soul and partial souls. The ratios are the same in all souls but the terms differ, and 'the remainder of the former things' means the last parts of the middle genera.","ideas":["Supermundane souls may stand between intellect and mundane souls","Essence, sameness and difference in turn mark divine, dæmoniacal and partial souls","Soul is defined as a middle essence from the middle genera","All souls share ratios but differ in terms"],"terms":[{"t":"entelecheia","d":"Aristotle's 'actuality' of a body, which Proclus rejects as a definition of soul."},{"t":"middle genera","d":"Essence, same and different, mixed between the impartible and the partible to make soul."}]},{"i":1968,"j":1973,"loc":"Book V, on Tim. 41d–e","title":"Second and third order; souls equal to the stars","summary":"On the lemma that the mixtures were no longer pure and incorruptible but of the second and third order, Proclus says partial souls are like total ones yet lesser, having more difference. Purity means being unchanging, not bending toward lower things, and free of Fate; partial souls are incorruptible in essence only. He ranks four genera of souls: divine; always joined to the gods; descending but pure; and wandering to Tartarus and back under Justice. On the next lemma, where souls equal in number to the stars are distributed to each star, he explains that the Demiurgus places partial souls under divine leaders. 'Equal in number' is by analogy, as thirty is to three, since each divine soul's number unfolds into many souls.","ideas":["Partial souls are incorruptible in essence but not in their activities","Four genera of souls, from divine to far-wandering","Partial souls are grouped under the star-gods","'Equal in number' is meant by analogy, not one soul per star"],"terms":[{"t":"Fate","d":"The order of laws that governs souls in generation, from which divine souls are free."}]},{"i":1974,"j":1984,"loc":"On Timaeus 41d–e: souls equal to the stars; the vehicle","title":"Souls distributed to stars and given vehicles","summary":"Proclus explains that each leading god imparts its form of life to a train of partial gods, angels, daemons, heroes and souls, so that partial souls are equal in number to the stars by sharing their forms. He reports Acyllus's view that each partial soul has the same number of parts as its star-soul, and ranks partial souls fourth after the world-soul, the souls of the spheres and the stars. On the lemma about souls ascending into a vehicle, he rejects Theodorus's reading that the vehicle is the nature of the universe and the reading that it is a star. Following Iamblichus but correcting him, he holds that the Demiurgus himself makes the vehicle together with the soul, so that it is perpetual and always attached to the same soul.","ideas":["Each soul belongs to the series of a leading god and to one of its powers","Partial souls are 'partial' in a fourth rank below universal and intermediate souls","The vehicle is made by the Demiurgus with the soul and is not cut from cosmic wholes","Divine souls contain their bodies; partial souls ride in vehicles and can be overcome by them"],"terms":[{"t":"ochēma (vehicle)","d":"The starry, perpetual body attached to each soul, in which it ascends, descends and travels to Hades."},{"t":"partial soul","d":"A human-type soul that cannot always remain on high and is ranked under a divine soul."}]},{"i":1985,"j":1990,"loc":"On Timaeus 41e: the impartible in the soul; Nature shown to souls","title":"The soul's intellect and the showing of Nature","summary":"Proclus asks what the impartible essence above a partial soul is, since such a soul has no intellect of its own that would keep it always on high. He proposes that each partial soul hangs from a daemon and shares that daemon's intellect, its own impartible element being its paradigm or form contained in that intellect. He then asks how the Demiurgus shows souls the nature of the universe without turning them toward sensibles. His answer is that the Demiurgus contains the cause of Nature in himself, and, following the theologians, that Nature exists first in the vivific goddess, so souls are shown Nature's fountains in order to govern the world from above.","ideas":["The vehicle supplies the soul's 'divisible' life; opinion is the paradigm of sense","A partial soul's impartible element is its form in a daemonic intellect","Showing Nature means turning souls to Nature's cause in the Demiurgus and the vivific goddess"],"terms":[{"t":"vivific goddess","d":"The life-giving deity from whose back, per the Oracles, Nature is suspended."}]},{"i":1991,"j":1997,"loc":"On Timaeus 41e: 'He announced to them the laws of Fate'","title":"What Fate is and its laws in souls","summary":"Proclus calls this the Demiurgus's second speech, addressed to souls rather than to the junior gods. He rejects definitions of Fate given by Alexander, Aristotle, Theodorus and Porphyry, and calls Fate a series of causes and periodic production: Nature in its subject, but deified and suspended from the Demiurgus. He links this to the Statesman's myth, where Fate turns the world, and to Necessity in the Republic. He then ranks Adrastia, Necessity and Fate as intellectual, supermundane and mundane causes of order, and says the laws of Fate are written into souls so that each choice carries the soul to its fitting place.","ideas":["Fate is deified Nature, filled with divine and intellectual gifts","Adrastia, Necessity and Fate form a descending triad of lawgiving causes","The laws of Fate are inscribed in souls' essence, not imposed from outside"],"terms":[{"t":"heimarmenē (Fate)","d":"The ordered chain of causes governing the sensible world, dependent on divine providence."},{"t":"Adrastia","d":"The intellectual goddess who, in Orpheus, nursed the Demiurgus and gathers all divine laws."}]},{"i":1998,"j":2004,"loc":"On Timaeus 41e–42a: the first generation is one for all","title":"One common first descent for all souls","summary":"Proclus explains that souls are above Fate by essence but become mundane through their vehicles and allotments. Fate governs the 'semination' of vehicles under their leading gods, after which each soul has one fixed generation and makes further descents by its own choices. He reports that Iamblichus took the first generation to be the sowing of vehicles, while another took it as a single descent. He prefers Syrianus's view: every partial soul descends once in each cosmic period, and some more often. He sets out how souls differ by leading god, by chosen life and by how well they live it, and says they are sown among the visible gods so as to have them as saviours.","ideas":["No partial soul can remain on high for a whole cosmic period","Syrianus: one descent per period is the first law of Fate","Souls differ by god, power, life chosen and manner of living"],"terms":[{"t":"first generation","d":"For Proclus, the soul's first descent from the intelligible into a mortal life in a given period."}]},{"i":2005,"j":2013,"loc":"On Timaeus 42a: 'the more excellent genus … would be called man'","title":"Male and female in the first descent","summary":"Proclus asks why Plato makes the first descent into men and whether women can live a perfect life. Citing Diotima and the Sibyl, he argues that women can lead such a life and so souls must also be able to descend into women. Plato's order of man, woman, beast is a gradual decline like the decay of constitutions in the Republic. From the priority of the male he draws a corollary that the universe and human generation had no beginning in time. He adds that male and female exist in souls themselves, received from their leading gods, though masculine souls do not always become men.","ideas":["The virtues of men and women are common, so women can live perfectly","Man-woman-beast describes a gradual loss of life, not a fixed rule","No first descent in time is possible, so the cosmos is unbegotten","Sex difference exists in gods, souls and bodies alike"],"terms":[{"t":"apocatastasis (restoration)","d":"The soul's return to its original state on high."}]},{"i":2014,"j":2024,"loc":"On Timaeus 42a–b: sense, love, fear and anger; justice","title":"Mortal life, its passions and justice","summary":"Proclus says that when souls are joined to material bodies they need the mortal life, whose summit they already contain in the spirit. He distinguishes three vehicles, immaterial, simple-material and composite, with immortal, longer-lasting and perishing lives. He interprets the three boundaries of mortal life: sense arising only from violent affections, desire with pleasure and pain mixed in love, and anger. He ranks three kinds of sense from impassive common sense in the first vehicle down to the scattered sense of the body. Souls that master these passions live justly because they contain their governing principles, as Ulysses checked his anger.","ideas":["Three vehicles with three lives: immortal, lasting, perishable","Sense and phantasy share one essence, directed outward or inward","Sense comes first, then desire, then anger, in the growth of the body","Rule over passions requires containing their causes"],"terms":[{"t":"phantasia (imagination)","d":"The power that, turned inward, sees shapes in the spirit; turned outward it is sense."},{"t":"pneuma (spirit)","d":"The soul's subtle vehicle, seat of the summit of the irrational life."}]},{"i":2025,"j":2033,"loc":"On Timaeus 42b–d: the kindred star; the second generation","title":"Return to the star and falling into lower lives","summary":"The just soul's return to its kindred star is brought about by the soul itself, by Fate and by the gods. Proclus explains the 'proper time' through the thousand-year periods of the Phaedrus, and the kindred star as the god under which the soul and its vehicle were placed. He distinguishes this return, possible after one life, from full restoration to the intelligible, which takes three thousand years. The bad soul's 'second generation' into a woman marks a decline of life, and the soul may also fall into brutish life. He offers a literal or a symbolic reading of 'woman' and repeats his argument that soul, man and cosmos are unbegotten.","ideas":["Return to the kindred star differs from restoration to the intelligible","Happy life is life under beneficent daemonic leaders","'Woman' may mean literal birth or a symbol of weakened life","A first human must otherwise be born of a woman, so generation has no beginning"],"terms":[{"t":"kindred star","d":"The divine body under which a soul and its vehicle were first sown."}]},{"i":2034,"j":2046,"loc":"On Timaeus 42c–d: brute lives; release from labours","title":"Brutish lives and the soul's purification","summary":"Proclus holds that a human soul can enter brute life only by being bound by sympathy to an animal's life, not by becoming its soul, citing Thersites as an ape in the Republic. Brutishness lies beyond human vice, so a newly perfected soul cannot fall straight into it. Salvation from the cycle of generation is the soul's return to the circle of the same, achieved by cathartic virtue that strips away what generation added. He distinguishes a connate starry vehicle, a second vehicle of the irrational life made of the four elements, and the shell-like body. Against interpreters who abolish or immortalise the vehicle, he separates these clearly and says the telestic art purifies best.","ideas":["Human souls ride on animal lives rather than becoming animal souls","Vice must first be human before it becomes brutish","Three bodies: connate vehicle, generation vehicle, earthly body","Cathartic virtue and telestic fire free the soul from generation"],"terms":[{"t":"cathartic virtue","d":"Purifying virtue that separates the soul from passions and leads it to intellect."},{"t":"telestic art","d":"Ritual initiation, which Proclus says purifies the soul's spirit by divine fire."}]},{"i":2047,"j":2050,"loc":"On Timaeus 42d: the legislative promulgation","title":"The ten laws of Fate and evil","summary":"Here, Proclus says, Plato completes the first creation and begins the second. He contrasts the divine 'thesmos' with human law: the Demiurgus foreknows all effects in a stable way, and thesmos is divine order above law. He counts ten laws of Fate in the speech, matching the Pythagorean decad, from the sowing of souls to the final law of release. The laws are placed in souls so that self-moving souls guide themselves and the Demiurgus is not the cause of their wrongdoing, as Lachesis's prophet also says. Evil arises from partial causes as a deviation from existence, which 'future' in the lemma signals as adventitious.","ideas":["Thesmos is divine order, higher than law","Ten laws of Fate correspond to the decad","God is blameless; the choosing soul is the cause","Evil exists by parypostasis, not as a proper principle"],"terms":[{"t":"thesmos (sacred ordinance)","d":"Divine order embracing all laws, distinguished from law as the distribution of intellect."},{"t":"parypostasis","d":"A parasitic, derivative existence, the way evil subsists."}]},{"i":2051,"j":2062,"loc":"On Timaeus 42d: sown into earth, moon and other instruments of time","title":"Sowing souls in the instruments of time","summary":"Proclus argues that the sowing of souls is neither random nor imposed against their will but follows the divine intellect and the souls' own choices. It concerns vehicles placed under the junior gods, not the world-soul, which is a monad. He asks whether vehicles are sown about the fixed stars or only the planets and concludes they can rise to the fixed sphere. He distinguishes 'distribution', of souls alone and into stars, from 'sowing', of souls with vehicles and so of 'men'. Earth must therefore be a star through its ethereal vehicle. Man is defined as a soul using a body: immortal, intermediate or composite.","ideas":["Sowing is set by divine intellect and willed by souls","Distribution is incorporeal; sowing involves vehicles","Earth and moon are named as extremes of heaven and generation","Three 'men' answer to three kinds of body"],"terms":[{"t":"instruments of time","d":"The heavenly bodies whose periods measure time, here also the places souls are sown."}]},{"i":2063,"j":2073,"loc":"On Timaeus 42d–e: the junior gods and the mortal animal","title":"Why the junior gods and evil","summary":"Proclus offers several reasons why the cosmic gods are called 'junior': compared with the older invisible creation, because they keep generation young, because of Hebe and the Curetes, because Bacchus, their monad, is the young god, and most of all because their bodies exist in time. The Demiurgus hands them the shaping of mortal bodies and the rule over what the human soul still lacks, making them makers, fathers and guardians. He bids them govern the mortal animal as well as possible. Proclus denies that some heavenly gods are malignant: true evils are vices we cause ourselves, and celestial gifts become harmful only through bad reception.","ideas":["Junior gods are 'young' because always coming to be in time","Body as a whole is unbegotten; particular bodies are perishable","No heavenly god is malign; recipients pervert divine gifts"],"terms":[{"t":"junior gods","d":"The cosmic gods who carry out the second, partial creation."}]},{"i":2074,"j":2085,"loc":"On Timaeus 42e–43a: the Demiurgus abides; the children borrow parts","title":"The two creations and borrowed elements","summary":"Proclus interprets the Demiurgus 'remaining in his accustomed manner' as his eternal fixedness in the one, and his children as hearers, learners and intellectual perceivers of his order, like Bacchus completing what Zeus made. He sets out contrasts between the first and second creation: commanding and obeying, eternity and time, indissoluble and dissoluble bonds. The junior gods borrow fire, earth, water and air from the universe and must return them, so that wholes are never used up and generation and corruption go on forever. 'Receiving the immortal principle' means taking the soul in its first vehicle to rule the mortal animal.","ideas":["The lowest of the first creation touches the highest of the second","Borrowed parts must be repaid so the cosmic wholes endure","Death returns to the universe what was always more its own"],"terms":[{"t":"second fabrication","d":"The creation of mortal things by the junior gods, dependent on the Demiurgus's first creation."}]},{"i":2086,"j":2090,"loc":"On Timaeus 43a: thick-set invisible nails","title":"Nailing together the earthly body","summary":"Proclus reads this lemma as the making of the shell-like organic body, distinct from the simpler second vehicle composed of the four elements. The nails are tiny elements inserted into solid parts, and the melting together is a Vulcanian work of heat and moisture. In time-bound generation the imperfect comes first and the soul enters last, which shows that the universe, made soul first, was not generated in time. He holds that the immortal soul enters at birth, not with the seed. The soul's 'periods' are its two circles, so the whole soul descends, against Plotinus.","ideas":["Simple bodies are melted, then nailed, then vivified, then joined to soul","The rational soul enters the body at birth","The whole soul descends, contrary to Plotinus"],"terms":[{"t":"ostreōdes (shell-like body)","d":"The gross earthly body, likened to an oyster's shell."}]},{"i":2091,"j":2100,"loc":"On Timaeus 43a–b: the vast river; the six motions","title":"Bound in the river of generation","summary":"Proclus notes that Plato here leaves aside the myths of descent and judgement found in the Republic, Gorgias and Phaedo, since the Timaeus treats soul physically. Souls descend to imitate the gods' providence and to complete the cosmos with mortal rational beings, but suffer through choice, attachment and mixture of unlike natures. The river is all of generation; the souls' binding is the end of their descent; neither 'vanquishing nor vanquished' means soul and body keep their natures. The disorderly six motions arise from the three dimensions doubled by contrariety, a number the Pythagoreans give to the animated body.","ideas":["Descent imitates divine providence and fills out the cosmos","The river is the whole flux of generation, as Lethe in the Republic","Soul and body unite without either losing its nature","Six motions = three dimensions times opposition"],"terms":[{"t":"genesis (generation)","d":"The realm of becoming into which souls descend."}]},{"i":2101,"j":2113,"loc":"On Timaeus 43b–d: nutrition, sense and the two circles","title":"How sense disturbs the soul's circles","summary":"Proclus traces the soul's tumult to the nutritive and sensitive lives, which overwhelm newborns most. He compares the soul to someone who sees his reflection distorted in a stream and wrongly thinks he himself suffers. He gives an etymology for aisthēsis (sense). Sense fetters the circle of the same, the discursive circle, without twisting it, but twists the circle of the different, where false opinion arises. Against Plotinus and Theodorus and with Iamblichus, he denies that anything in us always thinks, citing the Phaedrus charioteer. The soul's essence always lives and moves while its powers and activities can err.","ideas":["The soul is impassive but mistakes its bodily image for itself","The circle of the same is bound; the circle of the different is distorted","No part of our soul stays always intellectually active","Essence, power and activity are affected differently"],"terms":[{"t":"circle of the same / different","d":"The soul's two circuits, one knowing intelligibles, one opining about sensibles."},{"t":"oiēsis (conceit of knowledge)","d":"False belief that one knows, located in the opining part."}]},{"i":2114,"j":2124,"loc":"On Timaeus 43d–44a: intervals twisted and fractured","title":"Twisting, fracture and inversion of the soul","summary":"Proclus holds that all harmonic ratios exist in every soul, divine, daemonic and partial, though in partial souls the terms are multiplied. These ratios are essences with knowing powers; essence stays intact while activities are twisted and powers broken, but only in the circle of the different. He compares this to the Phaedrus horses and names three passions: dissension, fracture and turning, giving contrary, oblique and supine motion. He illustrates them in reason, by self-contradictory or consistently false opinions, and in the irrational part, by the continent, the passive and the intemperate. The soul's essence can be dissolved only by its maker, which implies it is indestructible.","ideas":["Harmonic ratios are real essences in all souls","Only the Demiurgus who bound the soul could unbind it","Contrariety, obliquity and supineness are progressive disorders","Temperance, fortitude and justice correct them"],"terms":[{"t":"leimma","d":"The remainder interval in the soul's harmonic division."}]},{"i":2125,"j":2133,"loc":"On Timaeus 44a: the inverted man; senses seem to rule","title":"The upside-down soul and sense's rule","summary":"Proclus reads the image of a man standing on his head as the soul's worst condition, with the divine part enslaved and the lower part tyrannising. He lines up its stages with the corrupt constitutions of the Republic and works out the left-right reversal with compass directions. Such a soul lives like a plant and confuses same and different, taking pleasure for good, as when some take the moving sun as still. No circuit then leads; it is a battle of giants where the earthborn rule the Olympian. Sense only appears to rule, since it is itself governed by externals.","ideas":["Head buried, feet raised: reason subjected to the irrational","Stages mirror contentious, oligarchic, pleasure-loving and tyrannical regimes","The wise free their bound Prometheus","Sense rules only by deceiving reason"],"terms":[]},{"i":2134,"j":2150,"loc":"On Timaeus 44a–c: recovery, education and the plan ahead","title":"Recovery of reason and close of commentary","summary":"Proclus infers that the rational soul is present in infants but stifled, not added later. As the flow of growth subsides, the circles return to their paths and the person becomes wise, and he rejects Galen's view that soul follows bodily temperament: the body only hinders. Education by moral habit and by mathematics and dialectic makes the soul 'entire and same', curing ignorance, while one who neglects it goes lame and unfinished to Hades. Plato now turns from ethics to physiology: the body's parts and the mortal soul, under the providence of the gods. Man is a microcosm, and the commentary ends here; the rest is lost.","ideas":["Reason is present but sluggish in the newborn","The body impedes but does not produce wisdom, against Galen","Moral training and learning together make the soul whole","Man mirrors the cosmos part by part"],"terms":[{"t":"microcosm","d":"Man as a small world with intellect, reason and bodies analogous to the cosmos."}]},{"i":2151,"j":2176,"loc":"Additional Notes by the Translator: charioteer and horses","title":"Taylor's notes; Hermeas on the soul's chariot","summary":"Taylor's appended notes begin with brief glosses: the Pythagorean columns of opposites, Syrianus, Proclus on the three kinds of Sirens, Platonic dialectic and physical virtues. He then gives Hermeas's scholium on the Phaedrus chariot, which ranks charioteer and horses as powers, not essences or activities, since the soul's essence is never corrupted. Hermeas matches the charioteer to the power of being and the horses to the powers of same and different. He also offers alternative mappings, for instance opinion with anger and desire. He explains 'sub-winged' as having the power of wings without always using it.","ideas":["Charioteer and horses are the soul's powers","Same and different are horses in motion and circles in return","The wing is the soul's power to rise, never lost"],"terms":[{"t":"hypopteros (sub-winged)","d":"Having wings in power but not always in act, said of human souls."}]},{"i":2177,"j":2195,"loc":"Additional Notes: Hermeas on enthusiasm and the four manias","title":"Hermeas on divine inspiration and madness","summary":"Taylor translates Hermeas on the Phaedrus's four kinds of madness. True enthusiasm happens in the 'one of the soul' above intellect and discursive thought, though lesser inspiration can touch reason, imagination, anger and desire. Gods are the efficient cause, and bodily conditions are only auxiliary. Hermeas explains why inspiration is intermittent in people but lasting in consecrated statues. The four manias, musical, telestic, prophetic and erotic, act on the soul in order: harmonising its parts, making it whole, gathering it into one and joining it to the gods. Each also has an outward effect, and they depend on one another, as in Orpheus.","ideas":["Enthusiasm proper belongs to the one of the soul","Statues stay inspired; souls tire","The four manias form a ladder of ascent"],"terms":[{"t":"mania (divine madness)","d":"Inspiration from the gods: musical, telestic, prophetic or erotic."}]},{"i":2196,"j":2223,"loc":"Additional Notes: poets, Troy, Phaethon and short notes","title":"Roman poets, Troy and Phaethon allegories","summary":"Taylor argues from Hermeas that no Roman poet had true inspiration. He points to Virgil's opening of the Aeneid and to his Fourth Eclogue, where he sets himself level with the Muses. He then glosses Jupiter's sceptre of twenty-four measures and gives Hermeas's allegory of the Trojan War: Ilion is matter, Trojans are embodied lives, Greeks are rational souls, and Helen is intelligible beauty. Olympiodorus's reading of Phaethon as a comet follows. Brief cross-references on the earth and on prayer close the group.","ideas":["Inspired poets begin by invoking the Muse","Troy allegorises the soul's struggle with matter","Phaethon's fall is a comet's extinction"],"terms":[]},{"i":2224,"j":2253,"loc":"Additional Notes: the supercelestial place; Adrastea","title":"Hermeas on the supercelestial place and Adrastea","summary":"Taylor gives Hermeas on the Phaedrus's supercelestial place. Only inspired poets like Homer and Orpheus have celebrated it, and 'truth' there means the order of the Nights. Its three negations, colourless, shapeless and untouchable, place it above Heaven, the Cyclopes and the Hundred-Handers in Orphic theology. Hermeas also lists four sources of truth: the One, Phanes, Jupiter and the Sun. Short notes follow on souls carried round by Heaven and on time. A final note explains Adrastea, seated in Night's vestibule with drumsticks, as lawgiver of all divine and mundane laws.","ideas":["The three negations map onto three Orphic orders","Night holds the truth of the gods","Adrastea legislates; justice judges"],"terms":[{"t":"Night","d":"Orphic goddess identified with the supercelestial place and prophetess to the gods."}]},{"i":2254,"j":2272,"loc":"Additional Notes: the thousand years; nine lives (Phaedrus 248c–249a)","title":"Hermeas on the law of Adrastea and nine lives","summary":"Taylor quotes the Phaedrus passage on the law of Adrastea and then Hermeas's commentary. A soul that has seen something of reality stays unharmed for a period; falling comes from inability, failing vision, evil daemons and forgetting. Hermeas contrasts the nine lives of the Phaedrus, ranked by vision, with the Republic's chosen lives. He derives them from reason, anger, desire and nature, with imitative lives below, and links each to forms seen above. The thousand, three thousand and ten thousand years are read as symbols of degrees of perfection, not literal spans.","ideas":["The first descent is always into a man","Nine lives follow from the soul's parts and from imitation","Numbers of years symbolise measures of purification"],"terms":[{"t":"law of Adrastea","d":"The inescapable ordinance assigning souls their lives by what they saw."}]},{"i":2273,"j":2295,"loc":"Additional Notes: the happy vision; the horses' struggle (Phaedrus 250b–c, 248a–c)","title":"Hermeas on initiation and the struggling souls","summary":"Taylor quotes the Phaedrus on the soul's former vision of beauty and gives Hermeas's explanation. Beauty is light from the intelligible, and the terms telete, myesis and epopteia name stages of initiation. He then quotes the passage on souls whose horses fight. Hermeas divides human souls into three ranks: philosopher, political man and vulgar man, or temperate, continent and intemperate. He interprets trampling, lameness, broken wings and opinion-nourishment, and argues the whole soul descends. The meadow nourishing the wing is the generative power of forms, or the Nights.","ideas":["Initiation moves from purification to closed-eye vision to beholding","Three ranks of souls follow the gods with less and less success","Broken wings mean dulled activity, not lost power"],"terms":[{"t":"epopteia (beholding)","d":"The highest stage of the mysteries, where the initiate is set among what is seen."}]},{"i":2296,"j":2454,"loc":"Endnotes, Book I","title":"Translator's notes to the first book","summary":"Taylor's endnotes open with Ammonius' praise of his teacher Proclus, an anecdote of Dr. Charles Burney calling the Commentaries too much for him, and a passage from Proclus on the Parmenides about secret and public doctrines among Pythagoreans, Peripatetics, Parmenides and Zeno. Most notes then propose corrections to the printed Greek, identify Homeric quotations, or point to the Latin version of Leonicus Thomæus. Interpretive notes make Timæus analogous to the Demiurgus Jupiter and the other speakers to the three ruling fathers, and set out the ladder of divine orders from being itself down to the mundane Gods. Others explain the arithmetic behind the numbers of the Atlantic war and end with the Chaldean oracle against measuring the earth and stars.","ideas":["Most notes are textual emendations of the Greek","The dialogue's speakers mirror the demiurgic Gods","Defects ascribed to Gods, like Vulcan's lameness, signify transcendence","The Neith inscription's line about bearing the sun survives only in Proclus"],"terms":[{"t":"Demiurgus","d":"The maker of the universe, identified by Taylor with Jupiter at the end of the intellectual order."},{"t":"supermundane order","d":"The order of ruling Gods placed above the liberated and the mundane Gods."}]},{"i":2455,"j":2624,"loc":"Endnotes, Book II (first part)","title":"Notes on the Demiurgus and Aristotle","summary":"The notes to the second book continue the emendations and add short doctrinal glosses. Taylor says Aristotle rose no higher than intelligible intellect and treated metaphysics physically, as Plato treated physics metaphysically. He explains that matter comes from Phanes or animal itself rather than from the Demiurgus, and that the Demiurgus is the exempt monad of the supermundane order as the good is of the intelligible. A long note reads the wanderings of Ulysses, after Numenius and Porphyry, as the soul's passage across the sea of generation; others gloss Orphic lines on the belly of Jupiter and the word demas as wholeness.","ideas":["Aristotle's first cause is intelligible intellect","Ulysses' voyage is an allegory of the soul's return","Protogonus bounds the intelligible order, Jupiter the intellectual","Celestial and sublunary causes lie in Jupiter's belly"],"terms":[{"t":"Phanes (animal itself)","d":"The paradigm of the world at the extremity of the intelligible order, also called Protogonus."},{"t":"imparticipable","d":"Said of an intellect or monad that is not consubsistent with a soul or body below it."}]},{"i":2625,"j":2776,"loc":"Endnotes, Book II (second part)","title":"Notes on ideas, participation and darkness","summary":"A long note argues that Plato's idea of a bed in the Republic is the craftsman's forming reason, not an idea in the divine intellect, since otherwise the painter would be fourth from truth. Further notes explain that the Gods proceed from the first principle by ineffable unfolding, not by participation, and that soul is deified through intellect and body through soul. Taylor lists five ranks of being, places evil itself after non-being, and describes two kinds of darkness, one below light and one above it, citing Damascius. The section ends with glosses on Rhea, Proserpine, Orphic lines, and a remark that the arguments refute the modern belief in infinite worlds.","ideas":["No divine ideas of artificial things","Two kinds of darkness and of ignorance","Providence is an energy prior to intellect","Saturn is once beyond, Jupiter twice beyond, in the Chaldean oracles"],"terms":[{"t":"dianoetic power","d":"The discursive reasoning part of the soul, where the craftsman's forming principle resides."},{"t":"hyparxis","d":"The highest, existential summit of a divine nature, here its goodness."}]},{"i":2777,"j":2936,"loc":"Endnotes, Book III (first part)","title":"Notes on proportion and the world's body","summary":"The notes to the third book distinguish Jovian, Dionysiacal and Adonaic fabrication and then work through the arithmetic of the means that bind the elements. Taylor checks Proclus' examples of similar plane and solid numbers, corrects Gaston Pardies on similar planes, and regrets that Proclus' method of finding two means is lost. He glosses the golden chain as the series of unities reaching down to matter, the seven corporeal worlds of the Chaldeans, the Teletarchs and the Curetes. Later notes argue that the perpetual parts of the universe refute modern astronomy, distinguish the world as single from solitary, and identify Rhea and Juno as causes of the soul.","ideas":["Numerical means bind the elements","The golden chain extends from the one to matter","The world is single and also self-sufficient","Leonicus Thomæus' Latin supplies missing words"],"terms":[{"t":"sesquialter","d":"The ratio of one and a half to one, as 3 to 2."},{"t":"Teletarchs","d":"Gods at the extremity of the intelligible and intellectual order, per Taylor."}]},{"i":2937,"j":3086,"loc":"Endnotes, Book III (second part)","title":"Notes on the harmonic numbers of soul","summary":"Taylor explains that the soul, as self-motive, is both ungenerated and always becoming, and that a bond is a union. Many notes test the ratios in Proclus' account of the soul's composition: definitions of harmonic proportion, a short algebraic proof, sesquitertian and sesquioctave examples, and the root ratio called pythmen. A diagram lays out the terms from 384 to 10368 divided into sesquioctaves and leimmas, and a note compares Proclus' tritone with the modern ratio of 45 to 32. Later notes show that geometric proportion contains the arithmetic and harmonic, discuss the divisibility of ratio types, and list the seven parts 1, 2, 3, 4, 9, 8, 27.","ideas":["The soul is generated and ungenerated in different respects","The soul's scale fills double and triple intervals","Geometric proportion comprehends the other two means","Five regular figures and five centres in the world"],"terms":[{"t":"leimma","d":"The remainder interval left after tones are taken from a fourth, in ratio 256 to 243."},{"t":"sesquioctave","d":"The ratio 9 to 8, the musical tone."},{"t":"pythmen","d":"A ratio in its lowest terms, the root from which others arise."}]},{"i":3087,"j":3250,"loc":"Endnotes, Book III (third part)","title":"Notes on the soul's circles and motions","summary":"Taylor assigns number triads to the four elements and explains the duple and sesquialter ratios as procession and return. He notes why the Pythagoreans called the cube harmony and why 8 and 27 are images of stability. Glosses follow on the chiasmus in the form of the letter chi, on Rhea holding Juno and Vesta, and on the circles of the same and the different as the equinoctial and the zodiac. He omits a theory Iamblichus called artificial, marks where Thomæus' Latin version ends, and explains kinēma and noēma as the boundaries of motion and thought.","ideas":["Each element has its own number triad","Same and different correspond to equinoctial and zodiac","Forty-nine terms fill the soul's intervals","Proclus alludes to the Christians"],"terms":[{"t":"entelecheia","d":"Aristotle's term for the cause by which a moving animal is defined or bounded."},{"t":"logistikon","d":"The reasoning part, restored by Taylor in place of the printed logikon."}]},{"i":3251,"j":3362,"loc":"Endnotes, Book IV (first part)","title":"Notes on time and the planets","summary":"The notes to the fourth book gloss the telestæ as initiators skilled in theurgy and explain divine delight as our proper reception of good. Taylor defends Aristotle's claim that soul numbers time as referring to time participated by matter, and says Proclus targets Christians who used Plato for a world made and destroyed in time. A long quotation from Iamblichus explains why the sun is said to change form with each zodiac sign. Other notes cover Ptolemy's works, the Chaldean seven worlds and two solar worlds, a passage on planetary speeds missing from editions of the Timæus, and Taylor's claim that all mundane light from the sun rules out fixed stars being suns.","ideas":["Eternity and time in the Demiurgus' distributive reasoning","The sun is one though received variously","Text on the speeds of the seven bodies is missing from editions","Jeremiah's queen of heaven is quoted"],"terms":[{"t":"telestæ","d":"Initiators into the mysteries capable of theurgic operations."},{"t":"cosmocrators","d":"Planetary rulers, each a leader and monad but not a wholeness like its sphere."}]},{"i":3363,"j":3466,"loc":"Endnotes, Book IV (second part)","title":"Notes on fixed stars and the Gods","summary":"Taylor notes misplaced passages in the original and glosses the Gods called water-walkers and the scholiast's headings on the fixed sphere. On precession he quotes Simplicius reporting Ammonius' astrolabe observation of Arcturus, but sides with Proclus that brief records cannot outweigh Chaldean observations over vast periods. A long note says every sphere holds its own Jupiter, Neptune, Vesta and the rest, so all things are full of Gods, and lists the twelve Gods in four triads. Other notes concern the earth as oldest of the Gods, instruments such as the armillary sphere and astrolabe, satellites of the stars, heroic souls, and the Sibyl as mean between Gods and men.","ideas":["Taylor rejects the precession of the equinoxes","Each sphere contains all the Gods in its own manner","The azonic Gods are the liberated Gods","Fixed stars as well as planets have satellites"],"terms":[{"t":"azonic Gods","d":"The Chaldean name for the liberated order just above the mundane Gods."},{"t":"inerratic sphere","d":"The sphere of the fixed stars."}]},{"i":3467,"j":3627,"loc":"Endnotes, Book V (first part)","title":"Notes on the generation of the Gods","summary":"The fifth book's notes mark passages too corrupt to restore and attack moderns who think the ancient Gods were deified men. Taylor quotes the Egyptian doctrine that heaven is above and below, with the full Emerald Table of Hermes in Latin and English. He prints an Orphic fragment placing Saturn above Ocean, corrects Phorcys to Ocean after Hesiod, and sets out the four divine progressions and the ennead in each element. Further notes cover the thirty-six decadarchs, the mixed bond of the young Gods, Rhea, Juno and Vesta, the heroic souls, the rite of enthronement, Fate as Nature deified, and the Pythagorean definition of desire.","ideas":["What is below resembles what is above","Heaven and Earth, Ocean and Tethys, Jupiter and Juno form successive pairs","The mundane Gods do not deliberate","Each element's sphere has a leading God with satellites"],"terms":[{"t":"thronismos","d":"The enthronement rite of initiation, with mystics dancing round the throne."},{"t":"decadarchs","d":"Thirty-six Gods dividing the zodiac, mentioned by Julian."}]},{"i":3628,"j":3760,"loc":"Endnotes, Book V (second part) and scholia","title":"Notes on the Titans, vehicles and Phaedrus","summary":"The central note quotes Olympiodorus on the Phædo: the four Orphic kingdoms of Heaven, Saturn, Jupiter and Bacchus, humans formed from the soot of the Titans who ate Bacchus, and these kingdoms read as grades of virtue. Taylor adds that the soul in the body lives under this Titanic life. Further notes explain the aerial and ethereal vehicles, twofold ignorance, and Prometheus and Epimetheus as the rational and irrational parts. The last notes treat a set of scholia on the Phædrus, correcting their German editor, reading Helen as generated beauty, and ending with Taylor's complaint that no one now has leisure for philosophy.","ideas":["Olympiodorus: suicide is forbidden because our body is Dionysiacal","The four kingdoms signify kinds of virtue","The ethereal vehicle is a superficies without depth","The soul is eternal in essence, temporal in energy"],"terms":[{"t":"Titans","d":"Fabricators nearest to things, from whose remains humans are said to be made."},{"t":"twofold ignorance","d":"Being ignorant and not knowing that one is ignorant."}]}]});